xt","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Commutative key","locale":"en"},"4967":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-93","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"All operations at the key are pairwise independent; a *witnessed coeffect* carries that proof.","locale":"en"},"4968":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-94","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Component `ℭΓ`","locale":"en"},"4969":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-95","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Triple `(d, p, e)`.","locale":"en"},"4970":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-96","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Fiber","locale":"en"},"4971":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-97","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Instantiation `⟨d, p, e, π, σ, τ, θ⟩` (parent, own table, retirement flag, lifecycle state).","locale":"en"},"4972":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-98","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Lifecycle state `θ`","locale":"en"},"4973":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-99","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"`Inactive \\| Reloading(i,g,ω) \\| Active(g,ω) \\| Unloading(g,ω)`.","locale":"en"},"4974":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-100","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Committed view `ω`","locale":"en"},"4975":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-101","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Map from declared keys to the *names of providing fibers* at commit time.","locale":"en"},"4976":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-102","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Target view","locale":"en"},"4977":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-103","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"What `ω` should be now (`⊥` if retired or unsatisfied).","locale":"en"},"4978":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-104","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Guard (`relied_n`)","locale":"en"},"4979":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-105","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"L-Unload may fire only when no installed fiber's committed view names `n`.","locale":"en"},"4980":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-106","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Quiescent","locale":"en"},"4981":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-107","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Every fiber settled at its target.","locale":"en"},"4982":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-108","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Support set `A`","locale":"en"},"4983":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-109","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Fibers that are not retired, whose parent is supported, and whose declared keys are provided by supported fibers.","locale":"en"},"4984":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-110","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Inertia","locale":"en"},"4985":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-111","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Asynchronous hosts cannot abort an iteration in flight; only the *landing* alternative of L-Divert is available.","locale":"en"},"4986":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-112","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"System boundary","locale":"en"},"4987":{"id":"/en/docs/concepts-113","page_id":"/en/docs/concepts","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/concepts#glossary","content":"Locations the system can exclusively modify and restore lie inside; others lie outside (emissions cross it).","locale":"en"},"4988":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","type":"page","content":"Concurrency and execution model","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","locale":"en"},"4989":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","content":"How kardia reproduces JavaScript's run-to-completion atomicity with one lock per root, critical sections that return actions, and spawned transitions.","locale":"en"},"4990":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#why","content":"Why the model matters","locale":"en"},"4991":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Chosen model","locale":"en"},"4992":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#lock-ordering","content":"Lock ordering","locale":"en"},"4993":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","content":"This page is decision **D1** of the guiding decisions: a `Send + Sync` core with one state lock per root, in which no user code runs while the lock is held.","locale":"en"},"4994":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#why","content":"Cordis's correctness rests on JavaScript's run-to-completion semantics: code between two `await`s is atomic. Many algorithms (inertia in `_setEpoch`, `_initTask ??=` in the loader, \"assign options synchronously then commit\") depend on this. A Rust port must reproduce *that atomicity*, not JS's single-threadedness per se.","locale":"en"},"4995":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"All mutable runtime state of one root lives in `Root { state: parking_lot::Mutex<State>, … }`.","locale":"en"},"4996":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"**Porting rule:** every synchronous segment of the TS code becomes **one critical section** on `State`. Nothing awaits while holding the lock, and **no user code is called while holding the lock** (plugin `apply`, disposers, event listeners, availability checks).","locale":"en"},"4997":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Critical sections are written as functions `fn step(&mut State, …) -> Actions`, where `Actions` is a small list of deferred work: `Spawn(Transition)`, `Emit(InternalEvent)`, `Wake(Notify)`, `RunDisposers(Vec<Disposer>)`. A tiny driver executes the actions after releasing the lock. This keeps the state machine **pure and unit-testable** and removes the reentrancy hazards JS hides (in TS, `_updateState` synchronously emits `internal/status` to user listeners from inside the state machine).","locale":"en"},"4998":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Transitions (`reload`, `unload`) are spawned tokio tasks. The paper's footnote 2 anticipates this: *\"with lazy scheduling (e.g. … Rust futures) the host must spawn the task for it to progress.\"*","locale":"en"},"4999":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Handles (`Context`, `Fiber`, `Svc<S>`) are `Send + Sync`; plugin futures must be `Send`. Users can freely `tokio::spawn` inside plugins (and tie the `JoinHandle` to an effect).","locale":"en"},"5000":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"Tests run on a `current_thread` runtime with `tokio::time::pause()` for deterministic interleavings (the analogue of vitest fake timers); production may use the multi-threaded runtime. Testing describes which runtime each test suite uses today.","locale":"en"},"5001":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"In `kardia-core` (`root.rs`), the root is the private `RootInner { state: Mutex<State>, changed: Notify, logger: Mutex<LoggerState> }`. A critical section is `RootInner::run(|st: &mut State| …)`: it pushes `Action`s onto `State::actions` instead of returning them. The actions are `Reload(FiberId)` and `Unload(FiberId)` (spawn the transition), `Emit(..)` (deliver an event to listeners), `Log { .. }` (report an error through the logger) and `Drop(..)` (values released under the lock are dropped after it, since their destructors are user code). Instead of per-action `Wake(Notify)`, every critical section wakes all waiters on the root-wide `changed` notifier after unlocking. Disposers are not run as an action: the unload transition runs them itself, outside the lock (see Lifecycle algorithms).","locale":"en"},"5002":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#model","content":"**Alternative considered:** a `!Send` design (`Rc<RefCell<..>>` on a `LocalSet`) mirrors JS most literally and was recommended by one analysis pass for the loader. It is rejected for the core because it would force every plugin and service in the ecosystem to be `!Send`-compatible (no `tokio::spawn`, awkward with most async libraries), while the lock-plus-actions discipline above preserves the same atomicity with comparable complexity. If profiling later shows lock contention on the access hot path, committed views (immutable per episode) can be published through `arc-swap` without changing semantics.","locale":"en"},"5003":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#lock-ordering","content":"`core::State` is the innermost lock. Higher layers (`loader` tree state, `include` IO state) have their own mutexes and may call into core only **after releasing** their own lock or while holding it **only if core never calls back into them under its lock** (which D1 guarantees).","locale":"en"},"5004":{"id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-concurrency","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-concurrency#lock-ordering","content":"Rule of thumb: *take at most one lock at a time*.","locale":"en"},"5005":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"page","content":"Contributing","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing","locale":"en"},"5006":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing","content":"How to work on kardia: building and testing, the xtask commands, CI, releasing to crates.io and npm, and the documentation site.","locale":"en"},"5007":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"Building and testing","locale":"en"},"5008":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"The npm packages","locale":"en"},"5009":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Repository tasks: `cargo xtask`","locale":"en"},"5010":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"CI","locale":"en"},"5011":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"Releases","locale":"en"},"5012":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"npm releases","locale":"en"},"5013":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"One-time setup","locale":"en"},"5014":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Secrets, variables and credentials","locale":"en"},"5015":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#site","content":"Documentation site","locale":"en"},"5016":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"Content","locale":"en"},"5017":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#deployment","content":"Deployment","locale":"en"},"5018":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing","content":"kardia is developed at github.com/licyk/kardia. This page covers the Rust workspace, the npm packages under `js/`, their CI and releases, and this documentation site.","locale":"en"},"5019":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing","content":"The repository's `AGENTS.md` has the code rules. In short: breaking refactors are allowed, with no compatibility layers; validate external data once, at the boundary, and tolerate unknown fields; no complexity for speculative races or edge cases without agreement first; keep main functions on the normal flow, with details in small helpers below; comment only non-obvious constraints; regenerate generated files instead of editing them; test the real implementation with few mocks; follow the repository's existing tools and workflows; extract a function only when it is reused, complex or a nameable concept.","locale":"en"},"5020":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"The WASM guest test builds its fixture as a WebAssembly module, so install the target first: `rustup target add wasm32-unknown-unknown`.","locale":"en"},"5021":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"Some tests build fixture crates with cargo while they run, so the first run takes a little longer.","locale":"en"},"5022":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"The minimum supported Rust version of the crates is 1.85 (`rust-version` in the workspace `Cargo.toml`); CI uses a pinned, newer toolchain (below).","locale":"en"},"5023":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#building","content":"How the tests are organised, and which runtime each suite uses, is on Testing.","locale":"en"},"5024":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"`js/` is a Bun workspace of the `@kardiajs/*` packages (TypeScript and Tauri), outside the Cargo workspace:","locale":"en"},"5025":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"The packages import each other by name. Type checking and tests resolve those names to the sources (`tsconfig.json` paths, `vitest.config.ts` aliases), so they need no build first; the build resolves them to the built packages.","locale":"en"},"5026":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"Inside a package, modules import each other by absolute path: `import { Fiber } from \"#/fiber\"`. `#/` is a subpath import, mapped in each `package.json` (`\"imports\": { \"#/*\": { \"source\": \"./src/*.ts\", \"types\": \"./lib/*.d.ts\", \"default\": \"./lib/*.js\" } }`). Node, TypeScript and bundlers resolve it per package, so the published `lib/` works as `tsc` emits it; type checking and tests resolve it to the sources through the `source` condition (`customConditions` in `tsconfig.json`, `ssr.resolve.conditions` in `vitest.config.ts`). The `@/` alias of `site/` would not survive into a published package, since `tsc` does not rewrite path aliases.","locale":"en"},"5027":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"The build copies the repository's `LICENSE` and `js/NOTICE` (the MIT notice of Cordis) into each package, since npm only ships files inside a package directory; the copies are gitignored.","locale":"en"},"5028":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#js","content":"Packages require each other as peers (`\"@kardiajs/core\": \"^0.1\"`), so an app has one copy of the runtime.","locale":"en"},"5029":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"The `xtask` crate (`xtask/`, a workspace member that is never published) holds the repository's own tasks; `.cargo/config.toml` makes them `cargo xtask <command>`.","locale":"en"},"5030":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Command","locale":"en"},"5031":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"What it does","locale":"en"},"5032":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask arch`","locale":"en"},"5033":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Checks the module structure: every `lib.rs` and `mod.rs` (in `crates/*/src`, `crates/*/tests` and `xtask/src`) only declares modules, re-exports, and holds docs and attributes. Implementation (functions, types, traits, `impl` blocks, constants, statics, `macro_rules!`, inline test modules) lives in a file of its own, re-exported from the entry file. Inline modules that only re-export (such as `kardia::prelude`) and proc-macro entry points, which Rust requires at the crate root, are allowed. It lists each violation with its file, line and kind, and fails if there is any. CI runs it.","locale":"en"},"5034":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask version <patch\\|minor\\|major> [--dry-run]`","locale":"en"},"5035":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Bumps the workspace version (`0.1.3` → `0.1.4` / `0.2.0` / `1.0.0`) in `[workspace.package]`, in the `kardia-*` entries of `[workspace.dependencies]`, and in `Cargo.lock`. When the requirement users write changes (a minor or major bump before 1.0, a major bump after), it also updates the `kardia = \"0.x\"` snippets of the README and these docs. It does not commit.","locale":"en"},"5036":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask tag [--version X.Y.Z \\| --name NAME] [--message TEXT] [--push] [--remote NAME] [--dry-run]`","locale":"en"},"5037":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Creates an annotated tag on `HEAD`: `v<workspace version>` by default. `--version` must equal the workspace version (a `v*` tag releases it), and `--name` creates any other tag. It refuses an uncommitted working tree and a tag that already exists, locally or (with `--push`) on the remote. `--push` pushes the tag (to `origin`, or `--remote`).","locale":"en"},"5038":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask js-version <patch\\|minor\\|major> [--dry-run]`","locale":"en"},"5039":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Bumps the version of the npm packages, which share one version of their own: `version` in each `js/packages/*/package.json`, the `^` ranges they require of each other when the requirement changes, and `js/bun.lock` (`bun install`). It refuses packages at different versions. It does not commit.","locale":"en"},"5040":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"`cargo xtask js-tag [same options as tag]`","locale":"en"},"5041":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#xtask","content":"Like `tag`, for the npm packages: `js-v<version>` by default, which starts the npm release.","locale":"en"},"5042":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"The repository has five workflows, each for one part of it, so that a change runs only the checks it needs and a release tests a commit once.","locale":"en"},"5043":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Workflow","locale":"en"},"5044":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Runs on","locale":"en"},"5045":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Does","locale":"en"},"5046":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`ci.yml` (Rust)","locale":"en"},"5047":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"pushes to `main` and pull requests, except changes only to `site/`, `js/`, Markdown, `LICENSE`, `NOTICE` and the hosting files; manual runs; called by Release","locale":"en"},"5048":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"fmt, clippy, `cargo xtask arch`, the test suite","locale":"en"},"5049":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js.yml` (npm packages)","locale":"en"},"5050":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"pushes to `main` and pull requests that change `js/` or `crates/kardia-bridge/`; manual runs; called by npm release","locale":"en"},"5051":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"the packages' types, tests and build; the bridge's end-to-end test against the Rust host","locale":"en"},"5052":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`site.yml` (docs)","locale":"en"},"5053":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"pushes to `main` and pull requests that change `site/`; manual runs","locale":"en"},"5054":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"the site's types, tests and build; outside pull requests, the deploy (below)","locale":"en"},"5055":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`release.yml`","locale":"en"},"5056":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`v*` tags; manual runs","locale":"en"},"5057":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"publishes to crates.io (below)","locale":"en"},"5058":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`npm-release.yml`","locale":"en"},"5059":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js-v*` tags (which `v*` does not match); manual runs","locale":"en"},"5060":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"publishes to npm (below)","locale":"en"},"5061":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"CI's steps:","locale":"en"},"5062":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Check out the repository.","locale":"en"},"5063":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Install Rust **1.99.1** (`dtolnay/rust-toolchain@1.99.1`) with `rustfmt`, `clippy` and the `wasm32-unknown-unknown` target. The toolchain is pinned because the compile-error snapshots of `kardia-macros` (trybuild tests) depend on the compiler version.","locale":"en"},"5064":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Restore the cargo cache (`Swatinem/rust-cache`).","locale":"en"},"5065":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`cargo fmt --all --check`","locale":"en"},"5066":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings`","locale":"en"},"5067":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`cargo xtask arch`","locale":"en"},"5068":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`cargo test --workspace`","locale":"en"},"5069":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"A newer push cancels an older CI run of the same branch or pull request. Tags never start CI.","locale":"en"},"5070":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"A change to","locale":"en"},"5071":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Runs","locale":"en"},"5072":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"a docs page (`site/`)","locale":"en"},"5073":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`site.yml` only","locale":"en"},"5074":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"the README, `AGENTS.md`, the licence","locale":"en"},"5075":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"nothing","locale":"en"},"5076":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"Rust code, tests, `Cargo.*`, `xtask/`, a workflow other than `site.yml` and the js ones","locale":"en"},"5077":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`ci.yml`","locale":"en"},"5078":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js/`","locale":"en"},"5079":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js.yml` only","locale":"en"},"5080":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`crates/kardia-bridge/`","locale":"en"},"5081":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`ci.yml` and `js.yml`","locale":"en"},"5082":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"a version bump, then its tag","locale":"en"},"5083":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`ci.yml` once; `release.yml` reuses that run","locale":"en"},"5084":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"an npm version bump, then its `js-v*` tag","locale":"en"},"5085":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#ci","content":"`js.yml` on the push, and again as the release's checks","locale":"en"},"5086":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"All crates share one version, `version` under `[workspace.package]` in the root `Cargo.toml` (the `[workspace.dependencies]` entries of the kardia crates carry it too).","locale":"en"},"5087":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"`cargo xtask version patch` (or `minor`, `major`), then commit and push: `git commit -am \"chore(release): kardia 0.1.1\"`, `git push`. The push runs CI.","locale":"en"},"5088":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"`cargo xtask tag --push` tags the commit `v0.1.1` and pushes the tag, which starts `.github/workflows/release.yml`.","locale":"en"},"5089":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"Release checks that the tag names the workspace version. It then finds CI's run of the tagged commit and waits for it if it is still running: a passed run is reused, a failed one stops the release. Only when there is no usable run (a commit CI never ran on, or a run a newer push cancelled) does Release call `ci.yml` itself as its checks. Each released commit is tested once.","locale":"en"},"5090":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"It publishes every crate whose new version is not on crates.io yet, in dependency order (`cargo publish --workspace`; `xtask` is never published), then creates a GitHub release for the tag, with notes since the previous `v*` tag.","locale":"en"},"5091":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"The publish step is **resumable**:","locale":"en"},"5092":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"Before each attempt, it asks the crates.io API which `name@version` pairs already exist and excludes those crates, so a crate is never published twice.","locale":"en"},"5093":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"crates.io lets an account create only a few new crates at once and then one every ten minutes. When `cargo publish` fails with `429 Too Many Requests`, the workflow waits 610 seconds and tries again, up to eight attempts; any other failure fails the step. A release that adds new crates waits out this limit.","locale":"en"},"5094":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"The workflow can also be started by hand (`workflow_dispatch`) on a branch to resume a release that stopped part-way.","locale":"en"},"5095":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#releases","content":"It publishes with the `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` repository secret.","locale":"en"},"5096":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"The `@kardiajs/*` packages share one version, apart from the crates' (they start at 0.1.0). The bridge's protocol has a version of its own (`PROTOCOL` on both sides): a client opening a session with another version is refused with an `invalid` error, so a host and a client from different releases fail clearly instead of misbehaving.","locale":"en"},"5097":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"`cargo xtask js-version patch` (or `minor`, `major`), then commit and push: `git commit -am \"chore(release): @kardiajs 0.1.1\"`, `git push`.","locale":"en"},"5098":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"`cargo xtask js-tag --push` tags the commit `js-v0.1.1` and pushes the tag, which starts `.github/workflows/npm-release.yml`.","locale":"en"},"5099":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"The workflow checks that every package is at the tagged version, runs `js.yml` as its checks, builds the packages, and publishes core, timer, bridge, tauri and vue, in that order, skipping a version npm already has (so a manual run resumes a release that stopped part-way). It then creates a GitHub release for the tag, with notes since the previous `js-v*` tag.","locale":"en"},"5100":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-releases","content":"It publishes with **npm trusted publishing**: the publish job's `id-token: write` permission lets it mint a short-lived OIDC token that npm accepts for packages whose trusted publisher names this repository and workflow. No npm token is stored anywhere, and npm attaches a provenance statement to each version automatically (this needs the repository to stay public).","locale":"en"},"5101":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**npmjs.com, organization.** Create the free organization `kardiajs` (the `kardia` package name and the `@kardia` scope belong to others), and add the maintainers' accounts. Enable two-factor authentication on them.","locale":"en"},"5102":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**npmjs.com, first publish.** npm configures a trusted publisher in a package's settings, so each package must exist first. From a machine logged in with `npm login`: `cd js && bun install && bun run build`, then `npm publish --access public` in `packages/core`, `timer`, `bridge`, `tauri` and `vue`, in that order. This publishes the first version without provenance; later versions come from CI. A package added later, such as `@kardiajs/vue`, needs this step, and its own trusted publisher, before its first CI release.","locale":"en"},"5103":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**GitHub, environment.** In the repository's *Settings → Environments*, create `npm`. Optionally restrict its deployments to `js-v*` tags and add required reviewers: the publish job waits for them.","locale":"en"},"5104":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**npmjs.com, trusted publishers.** In each package's *Settings → Trusted publishing*, add a GitHub Actions publisher: organization or user `licyk`, repository `kardia`, workflow `npm-release.yml`, environment `npm`. The fields are case-sensitive and npm checks them only at publish time. A new trusted publisher must publish within two days, or it expires and has to be recreated, so release a version (step 5) right after.","locale":"en"},"5105":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#npm-setup","content":"**First CI release.** Bump and tag as above. When it has published, set each package's *Publishing access* to \"Require two-factor authentication and disallow tokens\", so only the trusted publisher (and maintainers with 2FA) can publish.","locale":"en"},"5106":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Where","locale":"en"},"5107":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Name","locale":"en"},"5108":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Used by","locale":"en"},"5109":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"Notes","locale":"en"},"5110":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, repository secret","locale":"en"},"5111":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN`","locale":"en"},"5112":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`release.yml`","locale":"en"},"5113":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"crates.io API token with the publish scopes; existing","locale":"en"},"5114":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, repository secret","locale":"en"},"5115":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`GITEE_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY`","locale":"en"},"5116":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`site.yml`","locale":"en"},"5117":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"deploy key that pushes the site to `gh-pages`; existing","locale":"en"},"5118":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, environment","locale":"en"},"5119":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`npm`","locale":"en"},"5120":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-114","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`npm-release.yml` (publish job)","locale":"en"},"5121":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-115","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"holds no secret: it names the trusted publisher's environment and carries any protection rules","locale":"en"},"5122":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-116","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, repository variables","locale":"en"},"5123":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-117","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"none","locale":"en"},"5124":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-118","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"GitHub, workflow token","locale":"en"},"5125":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-119","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"`GITHUB_TOKEN`","locale":"en"},"5126":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-120","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"the release jobs","locale":"en"},"5127":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-121","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"provided by Actions; the jobs ask for `contents: write` (GitHub release) and `id-token: write` (npm) only where needed","locale":"en"},"5128":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-122","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"npmjs.com","locale":"en"},"5129":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-123","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"organization `kardiajs`, a trusted publisher per package, the 2FA-and-no-tokens policy","locale":"en"},"5130":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-124","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"npm","locale":"en"},"5131":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-125","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"configured on npmjs.com, not in GitHub","locale":"en"},"5132":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-126","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#credentials","content":"There is no `NPM_TOKEN`. If trusted publishing ever has to be bypassed, a granular npm access token limited to the `@kardiajs` packages, stored as an `npm` environment secret, would be the fallback; the workflow does not use one.","locale":"en"},"5133":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-127","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#site","content":"This site lives in `site/`: Fumadocs on TanStack Start (React), built with Vite and Tailwind CSS, with Bun as the package manager and script runner. It is outside the Cargo workspace, so `cargo` commands are unaffected.","locale":"en"},"5134":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-128","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#site","content":"`bun.lock` is committed; `bun install --frozen-lockfile` installs exactly the locked versions, as the deployment workflow does.","locale":"en"},"5135":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-129","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"Pages are MDX files in `site/content/docs/`, with flat slugs grouped by prefix (`guide-*`, `concepts-*`, `reference-*`, `development-*`).","locale":"en"},"5136":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-130","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"The site is bilingual. Chinese is the default language: `<slug>.mdx`, served without a prefix. English is `<slug>.en.mdx`, served under `/en/`. Every page exists in both languages, and a test checks it.","locale":"en"},"5137":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-131","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"The sidebar order and its section headings come from `meta.json` (Chinese) and `meta.en.json` (English). Both must list the same slugs in the same order; a test checks that too.","locale":"en"},"5138":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-132","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"Links between pages use `/docs/<slug>` (the site adds `/en` itself). Besides Fumadocs' defaults, pages can use the `Cards`, `Callout`, `Steps`, `Tabs`, `Accordions` and `Mermaid` components without imports.","locale":"en"},"5139":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-133","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#content","content":"Code, identifiers, configuration examples and the paper's notation stay as they are in both languages.","locale":"en"},"5140":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-134","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#deployment","content":"`.github/workflows/site.yml` checks and deploys the site; Rust's checks do not run for a docs-only change. A pull request that touches `site/` gets the types, tests and build. A push to `main` that touches `site/` also builds the site with `VITE_BASE_PATH=/kardia/` and publishes the output to the `gh-pages` branch (with the `GITEE_RSA_PRIVATE_KEY` deploy key), which GitHub Pages serves at **https\\://licyk.github.io/kardia/**. Deploys run one at a time.","locale":"en"},"5141":{"id":"/en/docs/development-contributing-135","page_id":"/en/docs/development-contributing","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-contributing#deployment","content":"The base path is only for GitHub Pages, which serves the site under `/kardia/`; without `VITE_BASE_PATH`, the build serves from `/`.","locale":"en"},"5142":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"page","content":"API design","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design","locale":"en"},"5143":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design","content":"The design of kardia's API: services, keys and checked access, caller attribution, effects and interruption, plugins and configuration, the lifecycle state machine, events, the logger and the built-in plugin crates.","locale":"en"},"5144":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#services","content":"Services, keys and access","locale":"en"},"5145":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#service-trait","content":"The `Service` trait","locale":"en"},"5146":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"Providing","locale":"en"},"5147":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"Accessing (Algorithm 6)","locale":"en"},"5148":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"Caller attribution (D4)","locale":"en"},"5149":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#mixins","content":"Associated properties and mixins","locale":"en"},"5150":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects","content":"Effects (D5, D6)","locale":"en"},"5151":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"API","locale":"en"},"5152":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"Semantics (from `fiber.ts:292-357`)","locale":"en"},"5153":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"Interruption and cancellation safety (D6)","locale":"en"},"5154":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugins","content":"Plugins, configuration and the lifecycle","locale":"en"},"5155":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"Plugin trait","locale":"en"},"5156":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"Configuration (D7)","locale":"en"},"5157":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"The lifecycle state machine (`lifecycle.rs`)","locale":"en"},"5158":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#instantiation","content":"Instantiation (O-Insert / O-Retire)","locale":"en"},"5159":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#replace","content":"Registry replace","locale":"en"},"5160":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#fiber-handle","content":"`Fiber` handle API","locale":"en"},"5161":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#events","content":"Events, logger and built-in services","locale":"en"},"5162":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Typed events","locale":"en"},"5163":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"Logger","locale":"en"},"5164":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"Built-in plugin crates","locale":"en"},"5165":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design","content":"This page covers the public API of `kardia-core` and the built-in plugin crates: services and access (decisions D3, D4), effects (D5, D6), plugins, configuration and the lifecycle (D7), and events, the logger and built-in services. The data structures these operate on are on Core data structures. For a user-oriented introduction, see the guide pages Plugins, Services and Effects.","locale":"en"},"5166":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design","content":"The signatures below are the plan's design. Most of them exist in the code under the same names; where a name, a parameter or a return type changed, a note under the sketch gives the current API. The deliberate behavioural departures are listed on Status and deviations.","locale":"en"},"5167":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#service-trait","content":"(Associated type defaults are not stable; the implementation will put `Meta` on a separate `trait Intercepted: Service` with a blanket \"no metadata\" default.)","locale":"en"},"5168":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#service-trait","content":"In the code there is neither a `Meta` type nor `Mergeable` / `Intercepted`. Interception metadata stays JSON: `trait Service: Any + Send + Sync { const NAME: &'static str; fn merge_intercept(values: Vec<Value>) -> Value { merge_shallow(values) } }`, where the values come outermost first and the default is a right-biased shallow merge of JSON objects. A service reads typed metadata with `ctx.intercept_meta::<T>(key)`. `#[derive(Service)]` (from `kardia-macros`) implements the trait; `#[service(name = \"database\")]` sets the key, which defaults to the hyphenated type name.","locale":"en"},"5169":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"Semantics follow `ReflectService.provide` exactly:","locale":"en"},"5170":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"a tracked effect;","locale":"en"},"5171":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"single source per realm (`AlreadyProvided { key, by }`);","locale":"en"},"5172":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"notify immediately only if the providing fiber is `Active`, otherwise on its transition to `Active`;","locale":"en"},"5173":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"the inverse removes the binding, notifies the realm, **waits until `dependents` is empty**, then removes the provision.","locale":"en"},"5174":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"In-place overwrite (`reflect.set`) is offered as `ctx.replace_value::<S>(..)` and, as in Cordis and the paper, is *not* observed by dependents.","locale":"en"},"5175":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#providing","content":"In the code, `provide` and `provide_arc` return `kardia_core::Result<EffectHandle>` (the error is `kardia_core::Error`, which wraps `KardiaError::AlreadyProvided` and the other variants). Further variants: `provide_as` / `provide_arc_as` (any value under an explicit key), `provide_checked` (with an availability predicate) and `provide_serialized_as` (a value with a JSON view for expressions and tooling). `ctx.replace_value` was not implemented: `KardiaError::NotProvided` and `ForeignBinding` are declared for it but no API returns them. Retiring a provider unbinds its live provisions at once (see Retired providers).","locale":"en"},"5176":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"`get` implements `locate`:","locale":"en"},"5177":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"own provisions in the same realm;","locale":"en"},"5178":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"walk fibers upward;","locale":"en"},"5179":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"stop at an isolation boundary;","locale":"en"},"5180":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"the first fiber whose `committed` has the key returns it;","locale":"en"},"5181":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"a fiber that declares the key without a committed impl → `Inactive`;","locale":"en"},"5182":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"reaching the root → root has **unchecked** access to the live store (as in Cordis).","locale":"en"},"5183":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"Reading the committed view (not the store) keeps a dependency readable during the consumer's own teardown (Theorem 70).","locale":"en"},"5184":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"Typed declarations: `Plugin::Inject` can be a tuple of service types (`(Database, Cache)`), implemented with `macro_rules!` over tuples, producing both the runtime `InjectSpec` and a typed accessor `ctx.deps::<P::Inject>() -> Result<(Svc<Database>, Svc<Cache>)>`. This is the first, macro-free step towards the compile-time mediation the paper suggests (§5.1.4, §6.4).","locale":"en"},"5185":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#accessing","content":"In the code: `get` and `AccessError` are as sketched. `try_get_live` is `live::<S>()` (and `live_as::<T>(key)`); `get_dyn` is `get_as::<T>(key)` for a typed value under a name, and `view(key)` for the JSON view of a service, which the loader's expressions use. Typed declarations use the `Deps` trait (implemented with `macro_rules!` for tuples of up to eight services): `Inject::of::<(Database, Cache)>()` builds the runtime declaration, and `ctx.deps::<(Database, Cache)>()` returns `(Svc<Database>, Svc<Cache>)`. `Plugin` has no `Inject` associated type; it declares with `fn inject() -> Inject`. The compile-time step is `Injected<D>`, used by `#[plugin]` (see Checked typed access).","locale":"en"},"5186":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"Effects created *on behalf of the caller* use `svc.caller().effect(..)`: they land in the caller's fiber, exactly what Cordis's tracker achieves with `this.ctx`.","locale":"en"},"5187":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"A service resolves *its own* dependencies through the `Context` it stored at construction (its def site), so no shadow contexts are needed.","locale":"en"},"5188":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"Service crates expose caller-aware APIs as **extension traits on `Svc<S>`** (allowed by coherence because the trait is local):","locale":"en"},"5189":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"Interception metadata is resolved at access time per Def. 27 / `resolveIntercept`: `[declared inject meta, outer…, inner]` merged right-biased.","locale":"en"},"5190":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#caller-attribution","content":"In the code, `Svc<T>` holds the value `Arc<T>`, the caller `Context`, the `key`, the `impl_id` and the providing fiber, with `caller()`, `key()`, `impl_id()`, `arc()`, `provider()` and `logger()`. `intercept()` is `meta() -> Value` (merged with `S::merge_intercept`) and `intercepts() -> Vec<Value>` (the unmerged list, declared `inject` metadata first). The timer's `TimerExt::timeout` returns `Result<EffectHandle>` and registers `self.caller().effect_sync(\"ctx.timeout()\", …)`. Service-to-service calls follow the def site, unlike TS (see Service-to-service calls).","locale":"en"},"5191":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#mixins","content":"Cordis's `foo.bar` associated services and `ctx.mixin` are JS-object conveniences. In Rust: a service named `foo.bar` is just another key; \"extending another plugin's type\" is done with extension traits (compile time) or with a registry service built on `IdTable` (runtime). No accessor/mixin layer is ported.","locale":"en"},"5192":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"In the code (`effect.rs`, `fiber.rs`):","locale":"en"},"5193":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"`Disposer` is a struct consumed when it runs (`Disposer::new(FnOnce())`, `fallible(FnOnce() -> Result<()>)`, `future(..)`, `try_future(..)`); its closure runs synchronously when disposal starts and may return a future that finishes the work. Panics are caught and reported as errors.","locale":"en"},"5194":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"Labels are `impl Into<String>`. `effect_sync` takes `FnOnce(&EffectScope) -> Result<()>`: the body collects its inverses on the scope instead of returning one. `effect` takes `FnOnce(EffectScope) -> Fut` with `Fut: Future<Output = Result<()>>`; the body is spawned. Both return `kardia_core::Result<EffectHandle>`.","locale":"en"},"5195":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"`on_dispose` returns `Result<EffectHandle>`; `ctx.defer(label, disposer)` registers an arbitrary `Disposer`.","locale":"en"},"5196":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-53","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"`ctx.checkpoint()`, `scope.checkpoint()` and `scope.yield_(d)` are synchronous: `ctx.checkpoint()?`, `scope.yield_(d)?`. `EffectScope` methods take `&self` (the scope is `Clone`), and there is a `defer_fn` shorthand; `adopt` takes `impl Into<EffectHandle>` (a `Fiber` converts).","locale":"en"},"5197":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-54","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-api","content":"`EffectHandle::dispose()` returns a `Disposal`, a future of `Result<()>` that can also be dropped; `ready()` returns a future of `Result<()>`. The handle also has `label()`, `is_active()` and `meta()`.","locale":"en"},"5198":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-55","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"`assert_active`: effects on a removed fiber fail with `KardiaError::InactiveEffect`.","locale":"en"},"5199":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-56","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"Inside one effect: disposers run **sequentially in reverse order**.","locale":"en"},"5200":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-57","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"Ownership transfer: a nested `EffectHandle` created inside an effect is registered on the fiber; `scope.adopt(h)` moves it under the outer effect (the TS `collect` → `_disposables.delete`).","locale":"en"},"5201":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-58","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"A failing effect body disposes what it collected; synchronous errors propagate to the caller, async ones are logged and reported via `ready()`.","locale":"en"},"5202":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-59","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"Fiber unload drains the accumulator in reverse insertion order and runs the disposers **concurrently** (`join_all`), logging failures and panics (`catch_unwind`), because Cordis tests require sibling effects to clean up concurrently (`service-dispose.spec.ts`). Ordered teardown is obtained by grouping steps in one effect.","locale":"en"},"5203":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-60","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#effects-semantics","content":"`EffectHandle` is a non-owning handle: dropping it does **not** dispose. Component lifetime is dynamic and governed by the fiber, which is exactly where the paper distinguishes Cordis from RAII (§7.3: lexical scoping is complementary, for resources local to one effect; inside a disposer, ordinary `Drop` types are fine).","locale":"en"},"5204":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-61","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"Rust *can* cancel a future by dropping it, something JS cannot, so a Rust host could offer the paper's *aborting* L-Divert. The plan deliberately does **not** drop in-flight `apply` futures: a future dropped between \"acquire resource\" and \"register its disposer\" would leak exactly what temporal composability promises to recover. Instead:","locale":"en"},"5205":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-62","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"the host is **inertial** (paper §4.4): a stale load lands, then unloads;","locale":"en"},"5206":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-63","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"explicit boundaries, `scope.yield_(..).await?` and `ctx.checkpoint().await?`, return `Err(Interrupted)` when the fiber's target changed since the load began (the TS `while (runner.epoch === oldEpoch)` check). Returning `Interrupted` from `apply` is not a failure; the fiber unloads what it registered.","locale":"en"},"5207":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-64","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"This is the loader's outer-fiber pattern (`yield () => {}` after import) made explicit.","locale":"en"},"5208":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-65","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#interruption","content":"The boundaries are synchronous in the code (`scope.yield_(..)?`, `ctx.checkpoint()?`). `ctx.checkpoint()` fails once the load has been superseded (the fiber's target no longer equals the target of its episode) or the fiber was retired; `scope.checkpoint()` fails once the effect has been disposed. Async effect bodies are spawned and never dropped half-way; disposal waits for the body to reach its end or a boundary.","locale":"en"},"5209":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-66","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"Erased form stored in runtimes: `PluginDef { key: RuntimeKey, name, inject: Arc<InjectSpec>, provide, decode: fn(&Value) -> Result<ErasedConfig, ValidationError>, apply: Arc<dyn Fn(Context, ErasedConfig) -> BoxFuture<'static, Result<(), PluginError>> + Send + Sync> }`. `RuntimeKey` is `TypeId::of::<P>()` for typed plugins and a pointer identity for closure plugins (dynamic modules add their own key kind, see Loader internals).","locale":"en"},"5210":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-67","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"In the code (`plugin.rs`, `registry.rs`):","locale":"en"},"5211":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-68","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"`Plugin` has `const NAME`, `type Config`, `fn inject() -> Inject` (default: empty), `fn provide() -> Vec<Key>` (default: empty) and `fn apply(ctx: Context, config: Arc<Self::Config>) -> impl Future<Output = Result<()>> + Send`. `Inject` (ordered keys, each with optional interception metadata) replaces `InjectSet` / `InjectSpec`.","locale":"en"},"5212":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-69","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"`ctx.plugin::<P>(config)`, `ctx.plugin_value::<P>(value)`, `ctx.plugin_def(&def, value)` and `ctx.inject(inject, f)` return `kardia_core::Result<Fiber>`; the closure of `inject` is `Fn(Context) -> Fut`. `ctx.plugin::<P>(config)` requires `P::Config: Serialize`: the typed config is serialized to a `Value` and decoded like any other configuration, so a `Deserialize`-only config is passed as JSON with `plugin_value`.","locale":"en"},"5213":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-70","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#plugin-trait","content":"`PluginDef` is a cheap clone of `Arc<{ key, name: Option<String>, inject, provide, tags, decode, apply }>`, built with `PluginDef::of::<P>()`, `PluginDef::new(apply)` or `PluginDef::typed(apply)` and refined with `.name(..)`, `.inject(..)`, `.provides(..)`, `.tag(..)`, `.validate_with(..)`. `RuntimeKey` is `Type(TypeId)` or `Def(u64)` (a counter-assigned identity for closure-based definitions). Dynamic modules reuse `Def`; they add no key kind of their own.","locale":"en"},"5214":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-71","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"Configuration arrives as `serde_json::Value` (from files, the loader API, or `plugin_value`) and is decoded **once** per (re)load attempt with `serde_path_to_error`, producing `ValidationError { issues: Vec<Issue { path, message }> }`, the analogue of Standard Schema issues. Unknown fields are tolerated unless the plugin opts into `deny_unknown_fields` (repository rule: tolerate unknown fields).","locale":"en"},"5215":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-72","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"`Fiber::update(value)`:","locale":"en"},"5216":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-73","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"validate first (a rejected config leaves the running instance untouched);","locale":"en"},"5217":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-74","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"emit `ConfigCommitted` (loader write-back) unless `no_save`;","locale":"en"},"5218":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-75","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"offer the new config to the plugin's **update hook**;","locale":"en"},"5219":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-76","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"if no hook accepts it, `restart()` (`revision += 1` → new target → unload → reload).","locale":"en"},"5220":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-77","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"Hook registration is an effect: `ctx.on_config_update::<P>(|new| async { …; Ok(Handled::Yes) })`.","locale":"en"},"5221":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-78","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#configuration","content":"In the code, `Fiber::update(value)` and `Fiber::update_with(value, no_save)` return `Result<Settle>`: validation errors are returned at once, and the `Settle` future resolves when the update has taken effect (`db.update(json!(..))?.await?`). The update hook is `ctx.on_update::<C>(|new: &C| -> bool)` (returning `true` takes the update over, no restart) or `ctx.on_update_with::<C>(..)` (returning the future that `update` resolves with); `C` is the plugin's decoded configuration type. Before a plugin's own decoding, the configuration passes through the root's config hooks (`ctx.on_config(..)`), which the loader uses to evaluate expressions.","locale":"en"},"5222":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-79","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"A faithful port of `_checkImpl` / `_refresh` / `_setEpoch` / `_reload` / `_unload` / `_updateState`, split into pure critical sections returning actions:","locale":"en"},"5223":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-80","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"`begin` sets the state, creates the `inertia` shared future, and, on leaving `Active`, emits notifications for every provision still bound (L-Leave before any inverse).","locale":"en"},"5224":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-81","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"`finish_reload`: if the target is unchanged → `Active` (or `Failed` if the apply errored; the error is recorded only if still current, so a superseded failure only cleans up); otherwise chain into `unload`.","locale":"en"},"5225":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-82","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"`finish_unload`: drop self from each committed impl's `dependents` and wake providers whose set became empty; then `Inactive` (→ `Pending`/`Failed`/`Disposed` by `settled_state()`) or chain into `reload` if the target is no longer ⊥.","locale":"en"},"5226":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-83","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"`wait_until` uses `Notify` with `Notified::enable()` registered *before* releasing the lock (no lost wake-ups).","locale":"en"},"5227":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-84","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#lifecycle","content":"The code splits the work slightly differently (`lifecycle.rs`). `refresh` computes the target and calls `set_target`, the port of `_setEpoch`, which records it and, unless the fiber has failed or a transition is in flight (`inertia: bool`), calls `begin_reload` (commit ω := resolved, register as dependent, record the episode, queue `Action::Reload`) or `begin_unload` (queue `Action::Unload`) and updates the state; `update_state` emits `StatusChanged` and, on entering or leaving `Active`, notifies the consumers of the fiber's live provisions. The spawned `reload` first yields (to coalesce superseded updates), then reads the plugin and its configuration (`prepare_execute`), resolves the configuration through the config hooks, runs `apply` with `catch_unwind`, and calls `finish_reload`. `unload` waits until no binding of the fiber has dependents, drains the accumulator newest first (`start_drain`), disposes all effects concurrently with `join_all`, logs failures, and calls `finish_unload`. `wait_until` registers `Notified::enable()` on the root-wide notifier before checking the predicate.","locale":"en"},"5228":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-85","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#instantiation","content":"`ctx.plugin(..)` creates `FiberData` (fresh `FiberId`, child `Scope`), resolves injections, and registers on the **parent** fiber an effect whose body inserts the fiber into its runtime and runs `refresh`, and whose inverse marks it removed, forces the target to ⊥, awaits the transition chain and settles to `Disposed` (TS `fiber.ts:178-206`). Unloading a parent therefore cascades to children through the parent's accumulator; re-enabling creates a *fresh* fiber (paper §4.4, Configuration).","locale":"en"},"5229":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-86","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#replace","content":"`registry.replace(old, new)` (aliases + `_replace` on each fiber: new runtime, new inject, re-resolve, clear error, `revision += 1`, refresh) is ported as is; it is the primitive behind HMR and is useful on its own (swapping an implementation behind live fibers).","locale":"en"},"5230":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-87","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#replace","content":"In the code: `ctx.registry().replace(&old_def, &new_def) -> Vec<Fiber>` returns the fibers moved to the new definition, and each moved fiber emits `Replaced`.","locale":"en"},"5231":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-88","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#fiber-handle","content":"In the code (`fiber.rs`): `context()` returns `&Context`. `settled()`, `restart()` and `fail(error)` return a `Settle` (a boxed future of `kardia_core::Result<()>`), `dispose()` a `Disposal` (a future of `Result<()>`; disposing the root fiber restarts it, reverting everything registered on it), and `update(config)` / `update_with(config, no_save)` a `Result<Settle>`. There are also `config()`, `inject()`, `error()`, `runtime()`, `def()`, `name()`, `parent()`, `is_removed()`, `is_root()`, `effect_handle()` and `effect_count()`. `ctx.fiber()` returns the fiber of a context.","locale":"en"},"5232":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-89","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Listeners are stored in an `IdTable` per event `TypeId`; registration is an effect of the listener's fiber.","locale":"en"},"5233":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-90","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Dispatch snapshots the table and skips removed listeners.","locale":"en"},"5234":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-91","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Optional realm filtering (`E: Scoped`) reproduces `Context.filter` for `ServiceChanged`.","locale":"en"},"5235":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-92","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Waterfall-style extension points (`internal/config`, `internal/get`, `internal/update`) are replaced by **explicit hook traits** (config transform hook for the loader, update hook per fiber, commit hook) rather than stringly-typed events.","locale":"en"},"5236":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-93","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"Internal events: `PluginInserted/Removed`, `StatusChanged { fiber, old, new }`, `ServiceChanged { key, realm }`, `ConfigCommitted { fiber, value }`, `Replaced { fiber }`.","locale":"en"},"5237":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-94","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#typed-events","content":"In the code (`events.rs`): `trait Event: Any + Send + Sync { type Output: Bailable; fn filter(&self, listener: &Context) -> bool { true } }`. `Bailable` (implemented for `()`, `bool`, `Option<T>` and `Result<T, Error>`) says which results stop a `bail` / `serial` dispatch and which count as failures of a `parallel` one. `filter` replaces `E: Scoped`: `ServiceChanged` reaches only listeners whose context resolves the key to the same realm. Listeners are kept in a `Vec` per `TypeId`. `on` returns `Result<EffectHandle>`; there are also `on_prepend`, `on_async`, `once` and `listener_count`. The hooks are `ctx.on_config(..)` (config transform), `ctx.on_update::<C>(..)` (update hook per fiber), and the `ConfigCommitted` event as the commit point. `ConfigCommitted` carries `{ fiber, config }`. `serial` returns `Result<Option<E::Output>>`: a panicking listener stops it with the panic as the error, while `emit` and `bail` log a panicking listener, synchronous or asynchronous, and continue (see Events).","locale":"en"},"5238":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-95","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"`Logger` is a core service:","locale":"en"},"5239":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-96","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"`ctx.logger(name)` → `Logger { name, level, fiber }` (name defaults to the calling fiber's runtime name, overridable by interception metadata `{ name, level }`);","locale":"en"},"5240":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-97","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"`Exporter` trait objects registered via `logger.exporter(e)` **as effects**;","locale":"en"},"5241":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-98","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"ring buffer of the last 1000 `Message { sn, ts, name, level, args }`.","locale":"en"},"5242":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-99","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"Format strings use Rust formatting (`format_args!`) instead of printf directives. A `tracing` bridge (a `tracing_subscriber::Layer` feeding exporters) can be added in `kardia-logger-console`; it is not required by core.","locale":"en"},"5243":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-100","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#logger","content":"In the code (`logger.rs`): `ctx.logger()` takes no name (it is named by `intercept(\"logger\", { name })` or after the fiber's plugin), `ctx.logger_named(name)` names it explicitly, and `svc.logger()` logs on a caller's behalf. Levels are `error`, `warn`, `info` and `debug`, and each takes `impl Display`, so `format_args!(..)` works directly. Exporters are registered with `ctx.add_exporter(Arc<dyn Exporter>)`, an effect of the current fiber; `Exporter::export(&Message)` and an optional per-name `level`. `Message` is `{ sn, ts, name, level, text, fiber }`: the text is already formatted (see Logger). The buffer holds 1000 messages by default (`ctx.log_buffer()`, `ctx.set_log_buffer_size(n)`). No `tracing` bridge has been added.","locale":"en"},"5244":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-101","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"Crate","locale":"en"},"5245":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-102","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"Port of","locale":"en"},"5246":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-103","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"Rust building blocks","locale":"en"},"5247":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-104","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`kardia-timer`","locale":"en"},"5248":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-105","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`plugin-timer`","locale":"en"},"5249":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-106","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`tokio::time::{sleep, interval}`, `JoinHandle::abort` as disposers; `timeout(d) -> Result<(), Disposed>` future; `interval(d) -> impl Stream`; `throttle/debounce` handles","locale":"en"},"5250":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-107","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`kardia-logger-console`","locale":"en"},"5251":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-108","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`plugin-logger-console`","locale":"en"},"5252":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-109","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"ANSI colours, label alignment, level map","locale":"en"},"5253":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-110","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`kardia-fs`","locale":"en"},"5254":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-111","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`plugin-fs-node`","locale":"en"},"5255":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-112","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"`notify` (watch parent dir for single files), `globset` + ported last-match-wins/ancestor predicate","locale":"en"},"5256":{"id":"/en/docs/development-design-113","page_id":"/en/docs/development-design","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-design#built-ins","content":"The timer API in the code (`TimerExt` on `Svc<Timer>`): `timeout(delay, callback)` and `interval(delay, callback)` are effects returning `Result<EffectHandle>`; `sleep(delay)` is the future that fails with `Disposed` if the caller is disposed first; `ticks(delay)` returns `Ticks`, whose `tick().await` resolves on the next tick (instead of a `Stream`); `throttle` and `debounce` return `Scheduled<A>` handles. `ctx.timer()` (`TimerContext`) is shorthand for `ctx.get::<Timer>()`.","locale":"en"},"5257":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"Core data structures","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","locale":"en"},"5258":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","content":"Identifiers, scope overlays, bindings, fibers, targets and committed views, IdTable and dependency-cycle detection in kardia-core.","locale":"en"},"5259":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#identifiers","content":"Identifiers (`key.rs`)","locale":"en"},"5260":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#scope","content":"Scope overlays (`scope.rs`): derived realization and live re-parenting","locale":"en"},"5261":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"Bindings (`store.rs`)","locale":"en"},"5262":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"Fibers (`fiber.rs`)","locale":"en"},"5263":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#id-table","content":"`IdTable<T>`: commutative-by-construction registry","locale":"en"},"5264":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#cycle-detection","content":"Dependency-cycle detection (D9)","locale":"en"},"5265":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","content":"The core data structures of `kardia-core`: the state the lifecycle runs on. Fibers live in a registry arena addressed by never-reused ids, and every cross reference (parent, dependents, committed view) is an id, never a pointer (decision **D2** of the guiding decisions). The algorithms that move a fiber through its states (refresh, reload, unload, instantiation) are on the API design page under The lifecycle state machine.","locale":"en"},"5266":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","content":"The sketches on this page are the plan's design. The code keeps their meaning but not always their shape; a note under each sketch gives the current names and fields in `crates/kardia-core/src`.","locale":"en"},"5267":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#identifiers","content":"Never-reused ids implement the paper's atomic generative names and make \"replaced by an equal value\" observable (Def. 53).","locale":"en"},"5268":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#identifiers","content":"In the code: `Key`, `RealmId`, `ImplId` and `FiberId` exist as sketched (`FiberId::ROOT` is `0`, `RealmId::new()` creates a fresh realm). `RealmId` prints as `realm#n`; the readable labels `svc#id` / `svc@label` belong to the loader's realms. There is no `EffectId`: effects are `EffectCell`s held in an `IdTable`, whose ids are plain `u64`.","locale":"en"},"5269":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#scope","content":"This replaces JS prototype chains (`Object.create`, `setPrototypeOf`, `swap`). Own vs. inherited is explicit.","locale":"en"},"5270":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#scope","content":"In the code, a `Scope` is one node of a context's derivation chain: `Scope { parent: RwLock<Option<Arc<Scope>>>, kind: ScopeKind }`, with `ScopeKind::{Fiber(FiberId), Isolate(Key, RealmId), Intercept(Key, Value), Overlay(Arc<Overlay>)}`. Every derived context (a fiber's own context, `isolate`, `intercept`, `extend`) adds a node; re-pointing a parent moves everything below it. The loader's mutable per-entry tables are public `Overlay` nodes (`set_isolations`, `set_intercepts`), and the entry marker is an `Overlay::tagged(tag)` found again with `Context::tag()`. Because scope nodes carry their fiber, the fiber chain of an access is read off the derivation chain (see Re-pointable contexts).","locale":"en"},"5271":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"The `type_id` check refers to paper §6.6.","locale":"en"},"5272":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"In the code, both structs live in `state.rs`:","locale":"en"},"5273":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"`Impl` has `key`, `realm`, `provider`, `provider_scope`, `value`, `check: Option<Check>`, `view: Option<View>` (a JSON reading of the value, for expressions and tooling) and `dependents`. It has no `id` (bindings are keyed by `ImplId` in `State::impls`), no `type_id` (a collision surfaces as `AccessError::TypeMismatch` when the value fails to downcast), and no `released` notifier (the unload guard waits on the root-wide notifier). `Check` is `Arc<dyn Fn(&[Value]) -> bool + Send + Sync>`: it receives the consumer's interception metadata for the key and runs under the lock, so it must not call into the runtime.","locale":"en"},"5274":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#bindings","content":"`State` has `fibers`, `runtimes: IndexMap<RuntimeKey, RuntimeData>` (registration order), `aliases: HashMap<RuntimeKey, PluginDef>`, `impls: HashMap<ImplId, Impl>` (every binding that still exists, live or withdrawn but still referenced), `store: HashMap<RealmId, ImplId>` (the live binding of each realm, the paper's Σ), `root_realms`, `listeners` (per event `TypeId`), `config_hooks`, id counters and the queued `actions`. There is no `props` table: accessors and mixins are not ported (see Associated properties).","locale":"en"},"5275":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`FiberState` is the paper's θ. A fiber in `Failed` recovers only through `update`, `restart` or `replace`.","locale":"en"},"5276":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"In the code (`state.rs` and `fiber.rs`), `FiberState` and `Target` are as sketched, except that `Target::Epoch::providers` is a `Vec<ImplId>` (the workspace does not use `smallvec`). `FiberData` differs:","locale":"en"},"5277":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"No `id` (it is the key of `State::fibers`), `runtime: Option<RuntimeKey>` (the root fiber has none), `inject: Inject`.","locale":"en"},"5278":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`config: Value` is the source configuration as given; it is decoded once per load attempt, not stored typed.","locale":"en"},"5279":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`episode: Option<Target>`: the target the current episode was loaded for, which the iteration checkpoints compare with.","locale":"en"},"5280":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`committed: Option<HashMap<Key, ImplId>>`; there is no `CommittedView` type.","locale":"en"},"5281":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`inertia: bool`: a transition is in flight. Waiting (`Fiber::settled()`, the unload guard) uses `RootInner::wait_until` on the root-wide notifier instead of a per-fiber `unload_gate` or `watch` channel.","locale":"en"},"5282":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`error: Option<Error>`.","locale":"en"},"5283":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`provisions: Vec<ImplId>`, plus `draining` and `withdrawn` (provisions withdrawn during a drain stay visible to the fiber's descendants until the drain is over).","locale":"en"},"5284":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#fibers","content":"`disposables: IdTable<Arc<EffectCell>>`, `update_hooks: IdTable<Arc<UpdateHook>>`, and `retire: Option<Arc<EffectCell>>` (the effect on the parent that instantiated the fiber, O-Insert).","locale":"en"},"5285":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#id-table","content":"The `DisposableList` design (insert returns a fresh id; remove by id; `drain_rev()` returns LIFO order) is exported publicly as `kardia_core::IdTable<T>`. It is what paper §3.4.2 calls a commutative key: service authors should build their registries (routes, listeners, commands) on it.","locale":"en"},"5286":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#cycle-detection","content":"On insertion, compute whether the precedence relation ≺ (`p_n ∩ d_m ≠ ∅`, from declared `provide`/`inject`) closes a cycle among non-disposed fibers in the same realm; if so, log a warning naming the cycle and leave the fiber `Pending` (behaviour otherwise unchanged). It only reads declarations, so it can be computed eagerly (paper §6.5). Declared `provide` is metadata only (as in Cordis); a plugin that provides undeclared keys simply escapes the check.","locale":"en"},"5287":{"id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-lifecycle#cycle-detection","content":"Implemented in `registry.rs` (`warn_cycle`): a depth-first search along \"depends on a declared provider\" edges, logging `dependency cycle: a -> b -> …` at warning level.","locale":"en"},"5288":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","type":"page","content":"Loader, include and dynamic modules","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-loader","locale":"en"},"5289":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader","content":"Internals of kardia-loader, kardia-include and kardia-hmr: the Rust adaptations of the Cordis loader, the file-backed include and the hot-reload algorithm with its module backends.","locale":"en"},"5290":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`kardia-loader`","locale":"en"},"5291":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"`kardia-include`","locale":"en"},"5292":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Dynamic modules and HMR (`kardia-hmr`)","locale":"en"},"5293":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader","content":"How the Cordis loader, include and HMR packages are ported: the data model, the entry state machine, file sync and the module graph. For how to use them, see the guide pages Loader, Hot reload and Guest plugins; for the TypeScript originals, see Loader, Include and group and HMR in the Cordis reference.","locale":"en"},"5294":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Port of `packages/loader` with these Rust adaptations:","locale":"en"},"5295":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5296":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Adaptation","locale":"en"},"5297":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Data model**","locale":"en"},"5298":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`EntryOptions` as a serde struct with `#[serde(flatten)] extra: IndexMap<String, Value>` (tolerate unknown keys); `Option<Option<T>>`/`Value::Null` for \"delete on merge\"; `IsolateLabel::{Private, Shared(String)}`; `Disabled::{Bool, Expr}`.","locale":"en"},"5299":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Id-addressed documents instead of object identity**","locale":"en"},"5300":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"TS relies on `entry.options === group.data[i]`; Rust keeps the group's `Vec<EntryOptions>` authoritative and entries hold ids; \"came from the file\" is a provenance flag, not `===`.","locale":"en"},"5301":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Entry state machine**","locale":"en"},"5302":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`update / _patchContext / _reconcile / init / _init / _start` ported 1:1 with the \"re-check after every await\" style (`fn still_current(&self, snap) -> bool`), `_initTask` as `Option<Shared<..>>`.","locale":"en"},"5303":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-14","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Two fibers per entry with `inject`**","locale":"en"},"5304":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-15","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"The outer dependency holder + the inner plugin are kept: the outer `apply` resolves the plugin from the catalog, hits `ctx.checkpoint().await?`, then starts the inner fiber.","locale":"en"},"5305":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-16","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Catalog**","locale":"en"},"5306":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-17","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Replaces module import: `Catalog::register::<P>()` / `register_alias(\"@cordisjs/plugin-group\", Group)`; a missing name yields `ModuleNotFound` located at the entry's `name`.","locale":"en"},"5307":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-18","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Managed realms**","locale":"en"},"5308":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-19","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`LocalRealm`/`GlobalRealm` as `RealmId`s with labels; the 7-step `patch-context` algorithm ported faithfully, with the delimiter test computed directly by walking scope parents (`scope.is_within(entry_marker)`) instead of temporary symbols; provider migration without restart; blocked moves tracked in `Impl::realm`. **Fix** the realm-GC early `return` (use `continue`).","locale":"en"},"5309":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-20","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Self-dispose / self-update write-back**","locale":"en"},"5310":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-21","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Core passes a `DisposeReason { SelfRequested, Parent, Loader }` instead of the TS `_disposing` flag + unregister-before-dispose trick (a deliberate breaking simplification).","locale":"en"},"5311":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-22","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**`loader` await barrier**","locale":"en"},"5312":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-23","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`Loader::settled().await` and an availability check driven by an atomic task counter.","locale":"en"},"5313":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-24","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Validation**","locale":"en"},"5314":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-25","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"`validate_options(&Value, deep) -> Vec<Issue>` at the boundary (deep for API calls, shallow for file input).","locale":"en"},"5315":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-26","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Expressions** (`!!js`)","locale":"en"},"5316":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-27","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"JS `with(ctx) eval` cannot be ported. Decision point (Risks): adopt a side-effect-free expression language (recommended: CEL, or a small custom evaluator) whose identifiers resolve through `ctx.get_dyn(name)` and a serializable service view (`trait ExprValue { fn to_value(&self) -> Value }`), respecting the same evaluation-context table as the TS loader. Until then, `Expr` values are rejected with a clear validation issue.","locale":"en"},"5317":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-28","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"Where `kardia-loader` differs from the table:","locale":"en"},"5318":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-29","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Data model:** `EntryOptions` is a typed view read from options already accepted by `validate_options` (`EntryOptions::read`); the JSON object stays the source of truth, so unknown keys survive write-back without a `flatten` map. `IsolateLabel::{Private, Shared(String)}` and `Disabled::{Bool(bool), Expr(String)}` are as planned.","locale":"en"},"5319":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-30","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Catalog:** `Catalog::register::<P>()`, `register_def(name, def)` and `alias(alias, name)` (not `register_alias`); `kardia_loader::builtins()` registers `group` and aliases `@cordisjs/plugin-group` to it, and `kardia::catalog()` adds the other built-in plugins under their names and their JavaScript package names.","locale":"en"},"5320":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-31","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Checkpoint:** synchronous, `ctx.checkpoint()?`.","locale":"en"},"5321":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-32","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Managed realms:** the delimiter test is `Context::is_within(..)` on contexts whose entry tables are tagged `Overlay` nodes; the realm-GC fix is in (`collect_realms` uses `continue`).","locale":"en"},"5322":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-33","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Self-dispose:** `DisposeReason` was not introduced. The loader sets a per-entry `disposing` flag around its own `fiber.dispose()` call (`entry.rs`, `dispose_fiber`), the same pattern as the TS `_disposing` flag.","locale":"en"},"5323":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-34","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Expressions:** implemented as a side-effect-free JavaScript subset (`kardia_loader::expr`) instead of CEL; identifiers resolve through the JSON views of services (`ctx.view(name)`, provided with `provide_serialized_as`), not through an `ExprValue` trait; see Expressions.","locale":"en"},"5324":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-35","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#loader","content":"**Key order:** options are `serde_json` maps, and `sort_keys` gives write-backs their stable order (`id`, `name`, the others alphabetically, `config` last) only with the `preserve-order` feature, which turns on `serde_json/preserve_order` and is on by default. Cargo unifies that feature into every `serde_json::Map` of a build, so it is optional: without it, maps and write-backs list their keys alphabetically. `kardia-include` and the `kardia` facade turn it on; `kardia-hmr` passes it through as a default feature, so an application that turns off `kardia-hmr`'s (and `kardia-loader`'s) default features keeps serde_json's default maps.","locale":"en"},"5325":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-36","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**Pure modules**, ported almost verbatim and unit-tested in isolation: `journal` (record, merge, flatten, apply, three-way `reconcile`; file wins), `patch` (insert/override patches, `PatchIndex` ownership, `route_journal`), anonymous id assignment.","locale":"en"},"5326":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-37","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**IO loop:** one task driven by `dirty_read/dirty_write` flags + `Notify`; latest-wins tree application; `flush()`.","locale":"en"},"5327":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-38","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**Parsing:** a YAML parser with spans and custom tags (`saphyr` family): `!!js` → `Value::Expr`; errors **and** warnings fatal; JSON via `serde_json` plus a spanned pass for locations.","locale":"en"},"5328":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-39","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**Atomic writes:** temp file in the same directory, stale-content check before every rename, bounded retry on `PermissionDenied`/`ResourceBusy`, read-only → keep journal in memory.","locale":"en"},"5329":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-40","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"**Errors:** structured `ConfigFrame { id, file, line, col }` chains instead of rewritten stack strings.","locale":"en"},"5330":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-41","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#include","content":"In the code, `serde_json::Value` has no expression variant: `!!js` becomes the object `{ \"__jsExpr\": \"…\" }` (`kardia_loader::EXPR_KEY`). Errors are `ConfigFileError` / `ConfigIssue` values whose locations are `file:line:col`, and the loader prints located frames as text (`at <id> (file:line:col)`) rather than a `ConfigFrame` type; see Errors.","locale":"en"},"5331":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-42","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Optional, Phase 7. Paper §6.4: *native code exposes no module registry, so introduction and retraction take the form of explicit dynamic linking (dlopen/dlclose) … WebAssembly … released when a native embedder (e.g. Wasmtime) drops it.* The plan separates the **algorithm** (portable) from the **module technology** (a backend):","locale":"en"},"5332":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-43","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"In the code (`graph.rs`): `trait ModuleSource: Send + Sync + 'static { fn load(&self, id: &str) -> Result<ModuleCode>; }`. `ModuleId` is a `String` (an absolute path), `ModuleCode { deps: Vec<ModuleId>, evaluate }` evaluates against its dependencies' exports, and a module exports `Exports::{None, Plugin(PluginDef), Value(Value)}`. `ModuleGraph::new(source)` keeps an ordered table of loaded records (`deps`, `exports`) and the set of host modules.","locale":"en"},"5333":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-44","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"The host owns a `ModuleGraph { records: HashMap<ModuleId, ModuleRecord { deps, kind: Host|Dynamic, instance } > }` replacing Node's caches.","locale":"en"},"5334":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-45","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"The Cordis reconcile algorithm is ported as pure graph code with tests from `reconcile.spec.ts`. This follows the **code**, not the paper's Algorithm 10 (see HMR: paper vs. code).","locale":"en"},"5335":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-46","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Transactions are serialized, and re-entrancy is rejected via a task-local transaction id.","locale":"en"},"5336":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-47","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Backends, in order of preference:","locale":"en"},"5337":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-48","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"**WASM components (wasmtime):** old and new instances coexist safely, dropping is sound, natural sandbox (paper §6.3 bridge fibers). Cost: services cross a WIT boundary.","locale":"en"},"5338":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-49","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"**dylibs (`libloading`):** development-time only: same toolchain, each version loaded from a unique copied path, **never unloaded** (unloading Rust dylibs is unsound with TLS destructors and leaked `'static` references), C-ABI entry point.","locale":"en"},"5339":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-50","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"**Out-of-process plugins** (respawn = reload) via the service-broker/RPC pattern of paper §6.2.","locale":"en"},"5340":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-51","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"Statically linked plugins are \"host modules\" by construction; changing them requires a process restart, while live *re*configuration remains fully supported by the loader.","locale":"en"},"5341":{"id":"/en/docs/development-loader-52","page_id":"/en/docs/development-loader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-loader#hmr","content":"All three backends exist: `WasmSource` (core WASM modules, not WIT components), `DylibSource` and `ProcessSource`, sharing one JSON guest ABI, plus `kardia-guest` for writing guests in Rust. Watch mode is `HmrConfig { base, watch }`. The full account of how they differ from this plan is under Dynamic modules.","locale":"en"},"5342":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","type":"page","content":"Risks and worked example","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-risks","locale":"en"},"5343":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-0","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks","content":"The risks and open decisions of the kardia design with their proposed answers, and a worked example of the plugin API.","locale":"en"},"5344":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-1","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Risks and open decisions","locale":"en"},"5345":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-2","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/development-risks#worked-example","content":"Worked example","locale":"en"},"5346":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-3","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Topic","locale":"en"},"5347":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-4","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Risk / question","locale":"en"},"5348":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-5","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Proposed answer","locale":"en"},"5349":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-6","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Threading model","locale":"en"},"5350":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-7","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"`Send` bounds everywhere vs. a literal JS-like `!Send` design","locale":"en"},"5351":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-8","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"`Send + Sync` with the lock-plus-actions rule (Concurrency); revisit only with evidence","locale":"en"},"5352":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-9","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Reentrancy","locale":"en"},"5353":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-10","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"user code called under the state lock deadlocks (`parking_lot` is not reentrant)","locale":"en"},"5354":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-11","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"enforced by structure: critical sections return `Actions`; availability `check` closures documented as runtime-free","locale":"en"},"5355":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-12","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"Disposal order","locale":"en"},"5356":{"id":"/en/docs/development-risks-13","page_id":"/en/docs/development-risks","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/development-risks#risks","content":"paper LIFO vs. Cordis concurrent fiber-lev