ontent":"calls listeners one after another, awaiting each, until one bails: `Ok(Some(..))` with its result, or `Ok(None)`","locale":"en"},"7079":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-191","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"`ctx.parallel(&event).await`","locale":"en"},"7080":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-192","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"calls all listeners concurrently and collects failures and panics into an `AggregateError` (`one or more listeners of event E failed`)","locale":"en"},"7081":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-193","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"Dispatch takes a snapshot of the listeners and skips those removed meanwhile. An event can restrict its audience with `filter`, which gets the context each listener was registered in.","locale":"en"},"7082":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-194","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"A listener that panics, synchronous or asynchronous, never takes the dispatching code down with it:","locale":"en"},"7083":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-195","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"Mode","locale":"en"},"7084":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-196","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"A panicking listener","locale":"en"},"7085":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-197","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"`emit`","locale":"en"},"7086":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-198","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"is logged (`panicked: <message>`); the rest still run","locale":"en"},"7087":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-199","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"`bail`","locale":"en"},"7088":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-200","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"is logged and counts as not bailing; the rest still run. An asynchronous one, which `bail` only starts, is logged when it panics","locale":"en"},"7089":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-201","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"`serial`","locale":"en"},"7090":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-202","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"stops the dispatch: `serial` returns `Err(..)` with the panic, and later listeners do not run","locale":"en"},"7091":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-203","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"`parallel`","locale":"en"},"7092":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-204","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"is one of the failures in the `AggregateError`; the rest still run","locale":"en"},"7093":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-205","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"A listener's own `Err(..)` result is an answer, not a failure: it bails `bail` and `serial`, which return it as `Some(Err(..))`.","locale":"en"},"7094":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-206","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#dispatching","content":"Unlike Cordis, `emit` does not let a failing listener abort the dispatch, and there is no generic `waterfall`: extension points that Cordis builds on waterfall events are typed hooks (below).","locale":"en"},"7095":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-207","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"The runtime emits these from the root, after each state change:","locale":"en"},"7096":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-208","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"Event","locale":"en"},"7097":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-209","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"Fields","locale":"en"},"7098":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-210","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"Emitted when","locale":"en"},"7099":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-211","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`PluginInserted`","locale":"en"},"7100":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-212","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`fiber`","locale":"en"},"7101":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-213","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"a fiber is created (`internal/plugin` on insertion)","locale":"en"},"7102":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-214","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`PluginRemoved`","locale":"en"},"7103":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-215","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`fiber`","locale":"en"},"7104":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-216","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"a fiber is retired (`internal/plugin` on removal)","locale":"en"},"7105":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-217","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`StatusChanged`","locale":"en"},"7106":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-218","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`fiber`, `old`, `new`","locale":"en"},"7107":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-219","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"a fiber changes state","locale":"en"},"7108":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-220","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`ServiceChanged`","locale":"en"},"7109":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-221","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`key`, `realm`","locale":"en"},"7110":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-222","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"a binding of `key` in `realm` appears or disappears; delivered only to listeners whose context resolves `key` to that realm","locale":"en"},"7111":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-223","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`ConfigCommitted`","locale":"en"},"7112":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-224","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`fiber`, `config`","locale":"en"},"7113":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-225","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"a fiber's configuration is replaced through `update` (the loader's write-back point)","locale":"en"},"7114":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-226","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`Replaced`","locale":"en"},"7115":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-227","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"`fiber`","locale":"en"},"7116":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-228","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#builtin-events","content":"a fiber is moved to another plugin implementation by `registry.replace`","locale":"en"},"7117":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-229","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"Where Cordis uses waterfall events (`internal/config`, `internal/get`, `internal/update`), kardia has typed hooks. Each registration is an effect:","locale":"en"},"7118":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-230","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"Hook","locale":"en"},"7119":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-231","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"Purpose","locale":"en"},"7120":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-232","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"`ctx.on_update::<C>(\\|config: &C\\| bool)`","locale":"en"},"7121":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-233","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"offers configuration updates of the current fiber to the hook before restarting it; returning `true` takes the update over (no restart). The hook sees the decoded configuration and is skipped if `C` is not the plugin's configuration type.","locale":"en"},"7122":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-234","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"`ctx.on_update_with::<C>(..)`","locale":"en"},"7123":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-235","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"the same; a hook that takes the update over returns the future that `update` resolves with","locale":"en"},"7124":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-236","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"`ctx.on_config(hook)`","locale":"en"},"7125":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-237","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"a configuration resolution hook (`ConfigHook`) for every fiber of the root: it gets the source configuration and the next stage, ultimately the plugin's own decoding. The loader uses it to evaluate expressions.","locale":"en"},"7126":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-238","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#hooks","content":"See Revertible effects for how `update` uses these hooks, and The loader for the loader's own hook.","locale":"en"},"7127":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-239","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"The logger is part of the core. `ctx.logger()` returns a `Logger` bound to the current fiber:","locale":"en"},"7128":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-240","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"**Name:** the `name` of the context's `logger` interception metadata if there is one; otherwise the plugin name of the fiber (or of its nearest named ancestor, else `root`), hyphenated (`fooBar` → `foo-bar`, `snake_case` → `snake-case`).","locale":"en"},"7129":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-241","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"**Level:** the `level` of the `logger` interception metadata, if any. `logger.with_level(level)` sets it on a copy.","locale":"en"},"7130":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-242","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`ctx.logger_named(name)` creates a logger with an explicit name (still taking the level from interception). `svc.logger()` logs on a caller's behalf (see above).","locale":"en"},"7131":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-243","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"Level","locale":"en"},"7132":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-244","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"Value","locale":"en"},"7133":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-245","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`Level::Error`","locale":"en"},"7134":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-246","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`0`","locale":"en"},"7135":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-247","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`Level::Warn`","locale":"en"},"7136":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-248","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`1`","locale":"en"},"7137":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-249","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`Level::Info`","locale":"en"},"7138":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-250","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`2`","locale":"en"},"7139":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-251","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`Level::Debug`","locale":"en"},"7140":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-252","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"`3`","locale":"en"},"7141":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-253","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#logging","content":"Messages take any `impl Display`; format with `format_args!`. Exporters therefore receive finished text: there are no `%`-directives or custom formatters as in Cordis.","locale":"en"},"7142":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-254","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#exporters","content":"An `Exporter` receives every message:","locale":"en"},"7143":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-255","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#exporters","content":"`ctx.add_exporter(Arc::new(exporter))` registers it as an effect of the current fiber. A message reaches an exporter if its level is at most `exporter.level(name)`, else the logger's level, else `Info`. A `Message` carries `sn` (a sequence number shared by all exporters receiving the same call), `ts` (milliseconds since the Unix epoch), `name`, `level`, `text` and the `fiber` that logged it.","locale":"en"},"7144":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-256","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#exporters","content":"The root keeps a ring buffer of the most recent messages at or below the logger's level (1000 by default): `ctx.log_buffer()` returns them oldest first, and `ctx.set_log_buffer_size(n)` resizes it.","locale":"en"},"7145":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-257","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#exporters","content":"The terminal exporter lives in `kardia-logger-console`: register `ConsoleExporter::new(ConsoleConfig { .. })` directly, or load the `logger-console` plugin from a config file (its options are listed in Configuration).","locale":"en"},"7146":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-258","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#exporters","content":"There is no `tracing` bridge; the plan leaves a `tracing_subscriber::Layer` feeding exporters as an optional addition to `kardia-logger-console`.","locale":"en"},"7147":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-services-259","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-services","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-services#example","content":"A longer, runnable version also hot-replaces the database implementation:","locale":"en"},"7148":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"page","content":"TypeScript and Tauri","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","locale":"en"},"7149":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-0","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"The @kardiajs npm packages: the same plugin model in TypeScript, the bridge that connects a TypeScript runtime, such as a Tauri webview, to a Rust kardia app, and extension points for Vue interfaces.","locale":"en"},"7150":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-1","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#model","content":"Two runtimes, one model","locale":"en"},"7151":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-2","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"The TypeScript runtime","locale":"en"},"7152":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-3","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#bridge","content":"The bridge","locale":"en"},"7153":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-4","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#host","content":"Host: `kardia-bridge`","locale":"en"},"7154":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-5","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#client","content":"Client: `@kardiajs/bridge` and `@kardiajs/tauri`","locale":"en"},"7155":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-6","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#client-services","content":"Services from the client","locale":"en"},"7156":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"Vue interfaces","locale":"en"},"7157":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"Where things belong","locale":"en"},"7158":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#protocol","content":"Protocol","locale":"en"},"7159":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"kardia composes a Rust app. When part of the app is TypeScript, as in a Tauri app whose interface runs in a webview, the `@kardiajs` packages give that side the same model: plugins, services, revertible effects and lifecycle. The **bridge** connects the two runtimes.","locale":"en"},"7160":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"Package","locale":"en"},"7161":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"Contents","locale":"en"},"7162":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/core`","locale":"en"},"7163":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"Contexts, fibers, revertible effects, reactive services, events and logging. No dependencies; runs in browsers and Node.","locale":"en"},"7164":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/timer`","locale":"en"},"7165":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"`timeout`, `interval`, `sleep`, `throttle` and `debounce`, cancelled when their plugin stops","locale":"en"},"7166":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-17","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/bridge`","locale":"en"},"7167":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-18","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"The client side of the bridge","locale":"en"},"7168":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-19","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/tauri`","locale":"en"},"7169":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-20","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"The bridge's transport over one Tauri command and a `Channel`","locale":"en"},"7170":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-21","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"`@kardiajs/vue`","locale":"en"},"7171":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-22","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"Vue integration: contribution points, `<ExtensionSlot>`, composables, routes","locale":"en"},"7172":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-23","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"`kardia-bridge` (crate)","locale":"en"},"7173":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-24","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","content":"The host side of the bridge, in the Rust app","locale":"en"},"7174":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-25","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#model","content":"Each runtime composes its own plugins. A Rust plugin cannot be a child of a TypeScript one or the reverse: an effect's inverse is a closure in one runtime's memory, and a service is a value there. So the bridge does not share a plugin tree. It keeps each side's view of the other in step:","locale":"en"},"7175":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-26","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#model","content":"**Effects never cross.** Each side reverts its own.","locale":"en"},"7176":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-27","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#model","content":"**Service availability crosses.** A service the host exposes appears on the client exactly while it is exposed, so a client plugin that injects it runs exactly then, and restarts when it is exposed anew.","locale":"en"},"7177":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-28","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#model","content":"**Calls are asynchronous** in both directions. A call into the other side fails with `unavailable` when that side is gone, and with `timeout` when it does not answer.","locale":"en"},"7178":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-29","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#model","content":"**Events** go from the host to every client, named.","locale":"en"},"7179":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-30","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#model","content":"This is the arrangement the paper describes for crossing a process boundary (each process hosts its own context, and calls between them follow an asynchronous contract, see Related work).","locale":"en"},"7180":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-31","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"It is a port of Kardia's core rather than a wrapper of the TypeScript Cordis:","locale":"en"},"7181":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-32","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"**Plugins** are functions, classes or objects with `apply`, each with optional `name`, `inject`, `provide` and `Config` (any Standard Schema validator). `apply` may return a promise, or a function that runs when the plugin stops.","locale":"en"},"7182":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-33","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"**Services** are keyed by string. `ctx.get(key)` is checked access: it fails for a service the plugin did not declare. `ctx.live(key)` reads the live store without a check. A `Service` comes back as a view whose `caller` is the accessing context, Kardia's `Svc::caller`.","locale":"en"},"7183":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-34","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"**Effects:** `ctx.effect(scope => ...)` collects inverses with `scope.defer` or returns one. An asynchronous body is never cancelled: `scope.checkpoint()` ends it once the effect is disposed.","locale":"en"},"7184":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-35","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"**Lifecycle:** fibers move through `pending`, `loading`, `active`, `unloading`, `failed` and `disposed`, as in Rust. A plugin restarts when a dependency is replaced, waits when one leaves, and its provider keeps running until its consumers have cleaned up. `fiber.update(config)`, `restart()`, `ctx.onUpdate(...)` and `ctx.registry.replace(old, new)` (hot replacement, for Vite HMR) behave as in Rust.","locale":"en"},"7185":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-36","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"**Events** are named: `ctx.on`, `emit`, `bail`, `serial`, `parallel`. The runtime reports `internal/plugin-inserted`, `internal/plugin-removed`, `internal/status`, `internal/service`, `internal/commit` and `internal/replace`.","locale":"en"},"7186":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-37","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"**Isolation and interception:** `ctx.isolate(key, realm?)`, `ctx.intercept(key, meta)`, `ctx.interceptMeta(key)`.","locale":"en"},"7187":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-38","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#core","content":"Where it differs from Kardia and Cordis is listed on Implementation status.","locale":"en"},"7188":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-39","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#host","content":"The app creates a `Bridge`, mounts its plugin, and routes client requests into `Bridge::handle`. Plugins inject `bridge` and expose services; an exposure is an effect of the exposing plugin, so it is withdrawn when that plugin stops:","locale":"en"},"7189":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-40","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#host","content":"`Bridge::handle` runs its work on the runtime the bridge plugin runs on, so a Tauri command can call it from Tauri's runtime. The Tauri glue is one command (the `kardia-bridge` crate docs show it in full):","locale":"en"},"7190":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-41","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#host","content":"Call `bridge.close_client(label)` when a window is destroyed. A reloaded page needs nothing: its new session replaces the old one with the same label.","locale":"en"},"7191":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-42","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#client","content":"Host services appear as `host.<name>` and host events as `host/<name>` (the `prefix` option changes `host`).","locale":"en"},"7192":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-43","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#client","content":"The client connects in the background and reconnects when the host's bridge restarts. Its plugin is active while disconnected; host services are simply absent. `bridge.status` and the `bridge/status` event report `connecting`, `connected`, `disconnected` or `replaced` (another webview with the same label took over; it does not reconnect).","locale":"en"},"7193":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-44","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#client","content":"A failed call rejects with a `BridgeError` whose `type` is `unavailable`, `timeout`, `invalid` or `failed`.","locale":"en"},"7194":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-45","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#client-services","content":"A client can provide a service to the host, as an effect of the calling plugin:","locale":"en"},"7195":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-46","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#client-services","content":"The host sees it as a `RemoteService`, provided by the client's session fiber and withdrawn when the session ends:","locale":"en"},"7196":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-47","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#client-services","content":"A client can close at any time, and the Rust app usually runs without any window open. So a host plugin should look a client service up when it needs it (`live_as`), rather than declare it in `inject`, which would stop that plugin whenever the window closes. The client provides its services again after every reconnect.","locale":"en"},"7197":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-48","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"`@kardiajs/vue` lets extensions contribute to a Vue interface: components, views, settings sections, commands, routes. A contribution is an effect of the plugin that makes it, so it leaves when that plugin stops, and the interface follows.","locale":"en"},"7198":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-49","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"**Contribution points** are `Contributions<T>` services (`defineContributions(key)` provides one). `register(item)` is an effect of the calling plugin; items are ordered by `order`, an `id` is unique, and `items` / `entries` are Vue-reactive.","locale":"en"},"7199":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-50","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"**`<ExtensionSlot name>`** renders the components of a point, in order. Each one gets the context of the plugin that contributed it, so inside it `useContext()`, `usePlugin()` and `useService()` act as that plugin, and its effects stop with it. `props` go to every component, under each contribution's own; the default slot shows while there is nothing; the `item` slot (`{ item, owner }`) renders each contribution itself.","locale":"en"},"7200":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-51","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"**Failing contributions** are contained: a contribution that throws (in setup, render, a hook, a watcher or a handler) is replaced by the `error` slot (`{ error, item, owner, retry }`), or by nothing without one, while the other contributions keep rendering and the error does not reach the app. `retry()` mounts it again. `@error` (`{ error, item, owner }`) reports the failure, for example to show which plugin failed; without it, the failure is logged by the owner's logger. The owner's plugin keeps running: a broken view is one contribution of the plugin, and stopping the plugin would also remove its services and its other contributions. To stop it, call `owner.fiber.dispose()` from `@error`.","locale":"en"},"7201":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-52","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"**Composables:** `useContext()`; `usePlugin(plugin, config)` runs a plugin for the lifetime of a component; `useService(key)` is a ref that follows a service (`undefined` while none is provided); `useContributions(key)` is a computed list for custom rendering, such as a navigation menu; `useFiberState(fiber)` follows a fiber.","locale":"en"},"7202":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-53","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"**Routes:** `addRoute(ctx, router, route)` adds a vue-router route as an effect: it is removed when the plugin stops.","locale":"en"},"7203":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-54","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"**Rust-only extensions** cannot ship Vue code, but a Rust plugin can expose a value describing its UI (a settings schema, say) and a TypeScript plugin can register a generic component for it.","locale":"en"},"7204":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-55","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#vue","content":"Third-party UI code runs with the webview's full access to Tauri: load only trusted code into the main webview, and give untrusted extensions declarative contributions or a sandboxed webview.","locale":"en"},"7205":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-56","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"Belongs to","locale":"en"},"7206":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-57","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"Backend capabilities, state, settings, persistence","locale":"en"},"7207":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-58","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"Rust plugins on kardia","locale":"en"},"7208":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-59","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"Interface composition: settings sections, commands, renderers, panels","locale":"en"},"7209":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-60","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"TypeScript plugins on `@kardiajs/core`","locale":"en"},"7210":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-61","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"Which host services a client can see, and calls into them","locale":"en"},"7211":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-62","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"the host's `expose`, through the bridge","locale":"en"},"7212":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-63","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"Transport, window identity, permissions","locale":"en"},"7213":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-64","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"Tauri (one command, one `Channel` per session, capabilities)","locale":"en"},"7214":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-65","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#responsibilities","content":"An extension can be Rust only (most backend capabilities; the interface renders what it exposes), TypeScript only (a panel or command using existing host services), or both: a Rust plugin that exposes a service, and a TypeScript plugin that injects `host.<name>`, so the interface half runs exactly while the backend half does.","locale":"en"},"7215":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript-66","page_id":"/en/docs/guide-typescript","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide-typescript#protocol","content":"The protocol (version 1) is JSON, tagged by `type`, with camelCase fields; unknown fields are ignored. The `kardia-bridge` crate docs list every request and message. Any transport works: the bridge's end-to-end tests drive the Rust host over stdin and stdout (`crates/kardia-bridge/examples/stdio_host.rs`).","locale":"en"},"7216":{"id":"/en/docs/guide","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","type":"page","content":"Guide","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Guide"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide","locale":"en"},"7217":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-0","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"How to use kardia: config files, plugins, services, effects, the loader, hot reload and guest plugins.","locale":"en"},"7218":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-1","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/guide#which-page-do-i-need","content":"Which page do I need?","locale":"en"},"7219":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-2","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"The guide explains how to build apps with kardia, from the config file of the `kardia` binary to plugins written in Rust and guest plugins that reload in place. It assumes kardia is installed; Quick start shows a running app first.","locale":"en"},"7220":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-3","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"<Card title=\"The configuration file\" href=\"/docs/guide-configuration\" description=\"Entries and their fields, expressions, two-way sync, and the built-in plugins.\" />","locale":"en"},"7221":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-4","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"<Card title=\"Writing plugins\" href=\"/docs/guide-plugins\" description=\"Services and plugins in Rust: #[derive(Service)], #[plugin], PluginDef, and registering plugins for the loader.\" />","locale":"en"},"7222":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-5","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"<Card title=\"Services\" href=\"/docs/guide-services\" description=\"Providing and using services, caller attribution, interception, events and the logger.\" />","locale":"en"},"7223":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-6","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"<Card title=\"Effects\" href=\"/docs/guide-effects\" description=\"Revertible effects, disposers, effect scopes and how a plugin is torn down.\" />","locale":"en"},"7224":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-7","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"<Card title=\"Loader\" href=\"/docs/guide-loader\" description=\"Entries, groups, isolation, include and the catalog, driven from Rust.\" />","locale":"en"},"7225":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-8","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"<Card title=\"Hot reload\" href=\"/docs/guide-hot-reload\" description=\"Reloading guest modules in place, and watch mode.\" />","locale":"en"},"7226":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-9","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"<Card title=\"Guest plugins\" href=\"/docs/guide-guests\" description=\"WebAssembly, dynamic-library and process guests, and the guest interface.\" />","locale":"en"},"7227":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-10","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide","content":"<Card title=\"TypeScript and Tauri\" href=\"/docs/guide-typescript\" description=\"The @kardiajs npm packages, and the bridge between a TypeScript runtime and a Rust kardia app.\" />","locale":"en"},"7228":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-11","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide#which-page-do-i-need","content":"**Running an app from a config file:** The configuration file, then Hot reload.","locale":"en"},"7229":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-12","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide#which-page-do-i-need","content":"**Writing plugins compiled into your app:** Writing plugins, Services and Effects.","locale":"en"},"7230":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-13","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide#which-page-do-i-need","content":"**Building your own host around the loader:** Loader.","locale":"en"},"7231":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-14","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide#which-page-do-i-need","content":"**Writing plugins that reload without restarting the app:** Guest plugins.","locale":"en"},"7232":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-15","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide#which-page-do-i-need","content":"**Adding a TypeScript side, such as a Tauri webview:** TypeScript and Tauri.","locale":"en"},"7233":{"id":"/en/docs/guide-16","page_id":"/en/docs/guide","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/guide#which-page-do-i-need","content":"The guide describes how to use kardia. Why it is designed this way is on Development, and the theory behind it on Concepts.","locale":"en"},"7234":{"id":"/en/docs","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"page","content":"kardia","breadcrumbs":["kardia Documentation","Getting started"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs","locale":"en"},"7235":{"id":"/en/docs-0","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"A Rust implementation of Cordis, the plugin framework built on spatiotemporal composability.","locale":"en"},"7236":{"id":"/en/docs-1","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#kardia-cordis-and-the-paper","content":"kardia, Cordis and the paper","locale":"en"},"7237":{"id":"/en/docs-2","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#how-this-documentation-is-organised","content":"How this documentation is organised","locale":"en"},"7238":{"id":"/en/docs-3","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#concepts-the-paper","content":"Concepts: the paper","locale":"en"},"7239":{"id":"/en/docs-4","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#cordis-reference-the-typescript-code","content":"Cordis reference: the TypeScript code","locale":"en"},"7240":{"id":"/en/docs-5","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#development-the-rust-design","content":"Development: the Rust design","locale":"en"},"7241":{"id":"/en/docs-6","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#suggested-reading-order","content":"Suggested reading order","locale":"en"},"7242":{"id":"/en/docs-7","page_id":"/en/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs#where-to-go-next","content":"Where to go next","locale":"en"},"7243":{"id":"/en/docs-8","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"kardia is a Rust implementation of Cordis, the plugin framework built on *spatiotemporal composability* (paper).","locale":"en"},"7244":{"id":"/en/docs-9","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"An app is a tree of **plugins**. Each plugin runs in a **fiber**:","locale":"en"},"7245":{"id":"/en/docs-10","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"**Effects:** everything a plugin sets up (listeners, timers, services, child plugins) is tracked and reverted when the plugin stops.","locale":"en"},"7246":{"id":"/en/docs-11","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"**Services:** plugins provide services and declare the services they depend on. A plugin starts once its dependencies are available, restarts when one is replaced, and stops when one goes away.","locale":"en"},"7247":{"id":"/en/docs-12","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"**Live changes:** you can reconfigure, replace or hot-reload any plugin while the app runs, and the rest of the app follows.","locale":"en"},"7248":{"id":"/en/docs-13","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"<Card title=\"Quick start\" href=\"/docs/quick-start\" description=\"Run the example app and change it while it runs.\" />","locale":"en"},"7249":{"id":"/en/docs-14","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"<Card title=\"Installation\" href=\"/docs/installation\" description=\"Requirements, crates.io and the list of crates.\" />","locale":"en"},"7250":{"id":"/en/docs-15","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"<Card title=\"Guide\" href=\"/docs/guide\" description=\"Configuration files, plugins, services, effects, the loader, hot reload and guest plugins.\" />","locale":"en"},"7251":{"id":"/en/docs-16","page_id":"/en/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs","content":"<Card title=\"Concepts\" href=\"/docs/concepts\" description=\"The paper: revertible effects,