"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-90","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"`loader/src/config/isolate.ts`","locale":"en"},"8261":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-91","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#correspondences","content":"plus migration of the provider's own impl without restart","locale":"en"},"8262":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-92","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#target","content":"**Paper:** `fiber.target` is a hash of `target(γ, n)` (key → provider uid tuple), ⊥ when unsatisfied.","locale":"en"},"8263":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-93","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#target","content":"**Code:** `_runner.epoch` = `\"<revision>:<uid>:<uid>…\"` or `'__INACTIVE__'`. The **revision** counter folds configuration revisions into the target: `restart()`, `update()` and `registry.replace()` bump it, forcing an unload→reload chain without retiring the fiber. This realizes the §4.4 *Configuration* revision more cheaply than retire → remove → reinsert at the same name.","locale":"en"},"8264":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-94","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#target","content":"See Target computation.","locale":"en"},"8265":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-95","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#retirement","content":"**Paper:** O-Retire (set τ) and O-Remove (drop the entry) are separate rules; a retired entry may linger (vestigial).","locale":"en"},"8266":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-96","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#retirement","content":"**Code:** the instantiation effect's inverse sets `uid = null` (removed from the runtime immediately), forces the target to ⊥, then awaits the transition chain; the final state is `DISPOSED`. There is no persistent registry of retired entries. Re-enabling an entry creates a **fresh fiber** (as the paper prescribes).","locale":"en"},"8267":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-97","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#retirement","content":"See Instantiation.","locale":"en"},"8268":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-98","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#guard","content":"**Paper (Algorithm 5):** `unload` awaits the `notify(...)`'d dependents' `await()`.","locale":"en"},"8269":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-99","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#guard","content":"**Code:** explicit reverse references `Impl.dependents: Set<Fiber>`, populated at commit time and removed at the end of the dependent's unload, plus wake-up resolvers (`impl.release`, `fiber._release`). Two guards exist: the **fiber-level** guard (no disposer of the provider runs until *all* its provisions are free) and the **binding-level** guard inside `provide`'s inverse. This gives the paper's per-binding semantics (`relied_n`) while letting a provider withdraw one binding without waiting for another.","locale":"en"},"8270":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-100","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#guard","content":"See The two guards.","locale":"en"},"8271":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-101","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#accumulator","content":"**Paper:** the accumulator is a LIFO composite; Algorithm 5 runs `fiber.dispose()` once.","locale":"en"},"8272":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-102","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#accumulator","content":"**Code:** fiber-level disposers are *initiated* in reverse order but run **concurrently** (`Promise.all`), each after one microtask; errors are logged and do not stop the others. Sequential LIFO holds only *inside* one `ctx.effect`. The paper acknowledges the concurrency (§5.1.3) and it is justified by Theorem 43 (independent inverses commute), but it means ordering-sensitive teardown must be grouped into one effect.","locale":"en"},"8273":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-103","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#accumulator","content":"See Fiber-level accumulator.","locale":"en"},"8274":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-104","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#iteration-boundaries","content":"**Paper:** the guard is checked before each iteration; L-Divert may abort or land.","locale":"en"},"8275":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-105","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#iteration-boundaries","content":"**Code:** only **async** iterators are interruptible; sync iterators and plain functions run to completion (they cannot be interrupted in a single-threaded host anyway). Async iterators take the *landing* alternative (an inertial host) and are closed with `iter.return()` so `finally` blocks run. `_reload` also yields one microtask before executing, so superseded loads never start.","locale":"en"},"8276":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-106","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#notification","content":"**Paper:** `notify` iterates all fibers and calls `refresh`.","locale":"en"},"8277":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-107","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#notification","content":"**Code:** it iterates all runtimes' fibers, filters by declared key and **realm symbol** (or a custom filter during realm moves), recomputes only the affected keys (`_checkImpl`), then calls `_refresh`. It also emits `internal/service`. Notifications for a fiber's provisions are issued from `_updateState` only on transitions into or out of `ACTIVE`.","locale":"en"},"8278":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-108","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#notification","content":"See `notify`.","locale":"en"},"8279":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-109","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#availability","content":"The code adds `Impl.check` (from `Service[symbols.check]`): a provider may report itself unavailable even while `ACTIVE` (used by `loader` for `{ await: true }`). This extends the satisfaction predicate `σ ⊧ d` with a per-provider condition.","locale":"en"},"8280":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-110","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#access","content":"`locate` additionally (a) lets a fiber read its **own** provisions without declaring them and (b) stops inheritance at an **isolation boundary**. The root fiber (no runtime) gets *unchecked* access (`reflect.get(prop, false)`), because root code cannot declare `inject`. See `locate`.","locale":"en"},"8281":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-111","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#caller-attribution","content":"The tracker/shadow proxy mechanism (Services and traceability) attributes effects created inside service methods to the **calling** fiber and resolves the service's own dependencies from its **def site**. The paper's confinement (Def. 55) implicitly assumes this; without it, service-mediated effects would belong to the provider and break temporal composability for consumers.","locale":"en"},"8282":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-112","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"See Loader.","locale":"en"},"8283":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-113","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"Two fibers per entry with `inject` (an outer dependency holder + the inner plugin).","locale":"en"},"8284":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-114","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"Expressions (`!!js`) evaluated in specific contexts; a cached `disabled` verdict.","locale":"en"},"8285":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-115","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"Self-dispose detection → persisted `disabled: true`; self-update → persisted config (`internal/commit`).","locale":"en"},"8286":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-116","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"The `loader { await: true }` barrier.","locale":"en"},"8287":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-117","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#loader-specifics","content":"Realm migration without restarting the provider; blocked moves tracked via `Impl.isolate`.","locale":"en"},"8288":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-118","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#hmr","content":"See HMR: Paper vs. code: registry-level in-place replacement (`registry.replace` + `_replace` + revision bump) instead of dispose + `ctx.use`; union-find commit units instead of all-or-nothing; no rollback after the point of no return.","locale":"en"},"8289":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-119","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Assumption","locale":"en"},"8290":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-120","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Status in the code","locale":"en"},"8291":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-121","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Effect witnesses (the inverse really reverts)","locale":"en"},"8292":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-122","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"unchecked, an author obligation (stated in the paper)","locale":"en"},"8293":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-123","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Coeffect commutativity","locale":"en"},"8294":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-124","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"unchecked, a provider obligation (stated in the paper)","locale":"en"},"8295":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-125","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Acyclicity of ≺","locale":"en"},"8296":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-126","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"**not detected**: a dependency cycle leaves fibers PENDING silently; the paper (§6.5) says a runtime *can* report it at load time, but Cordis does not","locale":"en"},"8297":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-127","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Totality on provision (Def. 76)","locale":"en"},"8298":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-128","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"not enforced; `provide` metadata on plugins is declarative only","locale":"en"},"8299":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-129","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"Confinement (Def. 55)","locale":"en"},"8300":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-130","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#unchecked","content":"enforced only for coeffect *access* through the proxy; arbitrary JS can still reach globals","locale":"en"},"8301":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-131","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#defects","content":"Location","locale":"en"},"8302":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-132","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code#defects","content":"Observation","locale":"en"},"8303":{"id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code-133","page_id":"/en/docs/reference-paper-vs-code","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/d