hich would stop that plugin whenever the window closes. The client provides its services again after every reconnect."],[7194,"A client can provide a service to the host, as an effect of the calling plugin:"],[6946,"a closure plugin"],[4405,"A coeffect at key `k` is a pair `(𝒱_k, 𝒜_k)`; each operation `a ∈ 𝒜_k` with argument `X_a` and outcome `B_a` acts **on the value alone**:"],[6788,"A compile error if `S` is not in `D`"],[6904,"A complete example"],[4631,"A component takes a configuration bound into its effect function; one component with two payloads is two components. The declarative layer may **revise** a running fiber (replace its config, reassign realms, disable or re-enable it). Disabling is O-Retire; every other revision is retire → deactivate → remove (children first) → reinsert at the same name. Re-enabling uses this composite rather than resetting `τ` to `⊥` (the monotonicity of `τ` underlies Theorem 80, and a re-enabled orphan would activate with its creator gone). In the implementation, re-enabling an *entry* instantiates a *fresh fiber*: the entry is the identity across revisions, the fiber the identity of one enablement. The shortcuts the loader takes (config handed to the component, a realm moved without reloading the provider) are justified by their *endpoint* (Theorem 80)."],[4847,"A component's loading is not one effect but a **sequence**."],[7125,"a configuration resolution hook (`ConfigHook`) for every fiber of the root: it gets the source configuration and the next stage, ultimately the plugin's own decoding. The loader uses it to evaluate expressions."],[4698,"A consumer is started only when every injected key is available; every provide/withdraw re-evaluates affected consumers."],[7523,"a consumer reads the **committed** impl, not the live store, so it keeps seeing its dependency during its own teardown (Theorem 70);"],[6302,"a crash brings down the host"],[6167,"a crashing guest fails only its own plugin"],[6724,"A custom `TreeBackend` get