first, and caches them. In a cycle, a module that is still being imported is seen as `Exports::None`. A module that fails to load is not kept, so its next import tries again."],[8135,"imports the module and patches the context;"],[5001,"In `kardia-core` (`root.rs`), the root is the private `RootInner { state: Mutex<State>, changed: Notify, logger: Mutex<LoggerState> }`. A critical section is `RootInner::run(|st: &mut State| …)`: it pushes `Action`s onto `State::actions` instead of returning them. The actions are `Reload(FiberId)` and `Unload(FiberId)` (spawn the transition), `Emit(..)` (deliver an event to listeners), `Log { .. }` (report an error through the logger) and `Drop(..)` (values released under the lock are dropped after it, since their destructors are user code). Instead of per-action `Wake(Notify)`, every critical section wakes all waiters on the root-wide `changed` notifier after unlocking. Disposers are not run as an action: the unload transition runs them itself, outside the lock (see Lifecycle algorithms)."],[7712,"In `registry.ts`, a **plugin** (the paper's *component*) is one of:"],[6027,"in a plugin's `apply`, once its load has been superseded or the fiber retired (the paper's L-Divert)"],[4684,"In a purely functional setting the two coincide; an imperative host may choose per operation. **Isolation and interception are given derived realization**: they produce a child context whose own table differs from the inherited one, leaving the shared table untouched, so there is nothing to track. Assignment on a derived table overrides the inherited one at that key, so neither operation has a precondition."],[4735,"In all cases loading is an effect whose inverses undo the registrations the module introduced."],[4626,"In an async host, iterations and inverses are futures that run to completion once started, so the **aborting** alternative of L-Divert is unavailable. Such a host is **inertial**: a fiber whose target turns during an iteration deactivates after that iteration lands. All results quantify over all runs, so they cover inertial runs; Theorem 73 never uses the aborting alternative. Inverses in flight need no counterpart (Unloading is never declined). A deactivation may chain directly back into an activation (implementation: `reload`/`unload` mutual chaining, see Implementation in the paper)."],[6029,"in an effect body, once the effect has been disposed"],[7041,"In configuration files, isolation is the entry field `isolate` (`{ db: true }` for a private realm, `{ db: shared }` for a realm shared under a label); see The loader."],[8122,"in place"],[5192,"In the code (`effect.rs`, `fiber.rs`):"],[5237,"In the code (`events.rs`): `trait Event: Any + Send + Sync { type Output: Bailable; fn filter(&self, listener: &Context) -> bool { true } }`. `Bailable` (implemented for `()`, `bool`, `Option<T>` and `Result<T, Error>`) says which results stop a `bail` / `serial` dispatch and which count as failures of a `parallel` one. `filter` replaces `E: Scoped`: `ServiceChanged` reaches only listeners whose context resolves the key to the same realm. Listeners are kept in a `Vec` per `TypeId`. `on` returns `Result<EffectHandle>`; there are also `on_prepend`, `on_async`, `once` and `listener_count`. The hooks are `ctx.on_config(..)` (config transform), `ctx.on_update::<C>(..)` (update hook per fiber), and the `ConfigCommitted` event as the commit point. `ConfigCommitted` carries `{ fiber, config }`. `serial` returns `Result<Option<E::Output>>`: a panicking listener stops it with the panic as the error, while `emit` and `bail` log a panicking listener, synchronous or asynchronous, and continue (see Events)."],[5231,"In the code (`fiber.rs`): `context()` returns `&Context`. `settled()`, `restart()` and `fail(error)` return a `Settle` (a boxed future of `kardia_core::Result<()>`), `dispose()` a `Disposal` (a future of `Result<()>`; disposing the root fiber restarts it, reverting everything registered on it), and `update(config)` / `update_with(config, no_save)` a `Result<Settle>`. There are also `config()`, `inject()`, `error()`, `runtime()`, `def()`, `name()`, `parent()`, `is_removed()`, `is_root()`, `effect_handle()` and `effect_count()`. `ctx.fiber()` returns the fiber of a context."],[5332,"In the code (`graph.rs`): `trait ModuleSource: Send + Sync + 'static { fn load(&self, id: &str) -> Result<ModuleCode>; }`. `ModuleId` is a `String` (an absolute path), `ModuleCode { deps: Vec<ModuleId>, evaluate }` evaluates against its dependencies' exports, and a module exports `Exports::{None, Plugin(PluginDef), Value(Value)}`. `ModuleGraph::new(source)` keeps an ordered table of loaded records (`deps`, `exports`) and the set of host modules."],[5243,"In the code (`logger.rs`): `ctx.logger()` takes no name (it is named by `intercept(\"logger\", { name })` or after the fiber's plugin), `ctx.logger_named(name)` names it explicitly, and `svc.logger()` logs on a caller's behalf. Levels are `error`, `warn`, `info` and `debug`, and each takes `impl Display`, so `format_args!(..)` works directly. Exporters are registered with `ctx.add_exporter(Arc<dyn Exporter>)`, an effect of the current fiber; `Exporter::export(&Message)` and an optional per-name `level`. `Message` is `{ sn, ts, name, level, text, fiber }`: the text is already formatted (see Logger). The buffer holds 1000 messages by default (`ctx.log_buffer()`, `ctx.set_log_buffer_size(n)`). No `tracing` bridge has been added."],[5210,"In the code (`plugin.rs`, `registry.rs`):"],[5276,"In the code (`state.rs` and `fiber.rs`), `FiberState` and `Target` are as sketched, except that `Target::Epoch::providers` is a `Vec<ImplId>` (the workspace does not use `smallvec`). `FiberData` differs:"],[5168,"In the code there is neither a `Meta` type nor `Mergeable` / `Intercepted`. Interception metadata stays JSON: `trait Service: Any + Send + Sync { const NAME: &'static str; fn merge_intercept(values: Vec<Value>) -> Value { merge_shallow(values) } }`, where the values come outermost first and the default is a right-biased shallow merge of JSON objects. A service reads typed metadata with `ctx.intercept_meta::<T>(key)`. `#[derive(Service)]` (from `kardia-macros`) implements the trait; `#[service(name = \"database\")]` sets the key, which defaults to the hyphenated type name."],[5645,"In the code, `ctx.checkpoint()` and `scope.yield_(d)` are synchronous checks (`ctx.checkpoint()?`, `scope.yield_(d)?`), not futures; the decision itself (no dropped `apply` futures, divert at explicit boundaries) holds. The `TypeId` of D3 is not stored with the binding: a key collision surfaces as `AccessError::TypeMismatch` when the stored `Arc<dyn Any>` fails to downcast."],[5221,"In the code, `Fiber::update(value)` and `Fiber::update_with(value, no_save)` return `Result<Settle>`: validation errors are returned at once, and the `Settle` future resolves when the update has taken effect (`db.update(json!(..))?.await?`). The update hook is `ctx.on_update::<C>(|new: &C| -> bool)` (returning `true` takes the update over, no restart) or `ctx.on_update_with::<C>(..)` (returning the future that `update` resolves with); `C` is the plugin's decoded configuration type. Before a plugin's own decoding, the configuration passes through the root's config hooks (`ctx.on_config(..)`), which the loader uses to evaluate expressions."],[5175,"In the code, `provide` and `provide_arc` return `kardia_core::Result<EffectHandle>` (the error is `kardia_core::Error`, which wraps `KardiaError::AlreadyProvided` and the other variants). Further variants: `provide_as` / `provide_arc_as` (any value under an explicit key), `provide_checked` (with an availability predicate) and `provide_serialized_as` (a value with a JSON view for expressions and tooling). `ctx.replace_value` was not implemented: `KardiaError::NotProvided` and `ForeignBinding` are declared for it but no API returns them. Retiring a provider unbinds its live provisions at once (see Retired providers)."],[5330,"In the code, `serde_json::Value` has no expression variant: `!!js` becomes the object `{ \"__jsExpr\": \"…\" }` (`kardia_loader::EXPR_KEY`). Errors are `ConfigFileError` / `ConfigIssue` values whose locations are `file:line:col`, and the loader prints located frames as text (`at <id> (file:line:col)`) rather than a `ConfigFrame` type; see Errors."],[5190,"In the code, `Svc<T>` holds the value `Arc<T>`, the caller `Context`, the `key`, the `impl_id` and the providing fiber, with `caller()`, `key()`, `impl_id()`, `arc()`, `provider()` and `logger()`. `intercept()` is `meta() -> Value` (merged with `S::merge_intercept`) and `intercepts() -> Vec<Value>` (the unmerged list, declared `inject` metadata first). The timer's `TimerExt::timeout` returns `Result<EffectHandle>` and registers `self.caller().effect_sync(\"ctx.timeout()\", …)`. Service-to-service calls follow the def site, unlike TS (see Service-to-service calls)."],[5270,"In the code, a `Scope` is one node of a context's derivation chain: `Scope { parent: RwLock<Option<Arc<Scope>>>, kind: ScopeKind }`, with `ScopeKind::{Fiber(FiberId), Isolate(Key, RealmId), Intercept(Key, Value), Overlay(Arc<Overlay>)}`. Every derived context (a fiber's own context, `isolate`, `intercept`, `extend`) adds a node; re-pointing a parent moves everything below it. The loader's mutable per-entry tables are public `Overlay` nodes (`set_isolations`, `set_intercepts`), and the entry marker is an `Overlay::tagged(tag)` found again with `Context::tag()`. Because scope nodes carry their fiber, the fiber chain of an access is read off the derivation chain (see Re-pointable contexts)."],[5272,"In the code, both structs live in `state.rs`:"],[5230,"In the code: `ctx.registry().replace(&old_def, &new_def) -> Vec<Fiber>` returns the fibers moved to the new definition, and each moved fiber emits `Replaced`."],[5185,"In the code: `get` and `AccessError` are as sketched. `try_get_live` is `live::<S>()` (and `live_as::<T>(key)`); `get_dyn` is `get_as::<T>(key)` for a typed value under a name, and `view(key)` for the JSON view of a service, which the loader's expressions use. Typed declarations use the `Deps` trait (implemented with `macro_rules!` for tuples of up to eight services): `Inject::of::<(Database, Cache)>()` builds the runtime declaration, and `ctx.deps::<(Database, Cache)>()` returns `(Svc<Database>, Svc<Cache>)`. `Plugin` has no `Inject` associated type; it declares with `fn inject() -> Inject`. The compile-time step is `Injected<D>`, used by `#[plugin]` (see Checked typed access)."],[5268,"In the code: `Key`, `RealmId`, `ImplId` and `FiberId` exist as sketched (`FiberId::ROOT` is `0`, `RealmId::new()` creates a fresh realm). `RealmId` prints as `realm#n`; the readable labels `svc#id` / `svc@label` belong to the loader's realms. There is no `EffectId`: effects are `EffectCell`s held in an `IdTable`, whose ids are plain `u64`."],[6162,"in the host process"],[5851,"In the parent group's context"],[6492,"In watch mode, `hmr` watches `base` through the `fs` service and turns file changes into transactions:"],[6711,"In YAML, `!!js <source>` reads as an expression object `{ \"__jsExpr\": source }` and is written back the same way. Unknown tags are errors, since they would leave the data incomplete. Written YAML uses block style, with single-quoted strings where needed."],[5174,"In-place overwrite (`reflect.set`) is offered as `ctx.replace_value::<S>(..)` and, as in Cordis and the paper, is *not* observed by dependents."],[1498,"include"],[3747,"include"],[4266,"include"],[5759,"include"],[8008,"include"],[8527,"include"],[8010,"include (internal)"],[5551,"include `sync.spec`, `patch.spec`, `reload.spec`, `write.spec` (fault injection on rename), `parse.spec`; fs `watch.spec`"],[1290,"include `sync.spec`、`patch.spec`、`reload.spec`、`write.spec`（在重命名时注入故障）、`parse.spec`；fs `watch.spec`"],[5422,"include + fs specs green; editing `kardia.yml` live reconfigures a running app"],[7285,"Include and group"],[7933,"