],[4545,"There are two levels of checking: the transition level (`reload`/`unload` check the target at completion, giving inertial chaining) and the iteration level (`execute` checks at every boundary, giving partial rollback)."],[4312,"There are two relations: **orchestration** rules `γ ⇒ δ` (actions the orchestrator *may* perform; the premises say when they are legal, not when they occur) and **lifecycle** rules `γ ⟶ δ` (the system takes them unprompted whenever their premises hold). Any interleaving is a valid run."],[4576,"There is genuine dependency topology (adapters, databases, features). Reconfiguring a provider reactivates only the dependents whose resolved dependency changed; a plugin with an unavailable dependency waits without erroring."],[6407,"There is no `cordis_call`, as the plugin provides no service."],[5132,"There is no `NPM_TOKEN`. If trusted publishing ever has to be bypassed, a granular npm access token limited to the `@kardiajs` packages, stored as an `npm` environment secret, would be the fallback; the workflow does not use one."],[7146,"There is no `tracing` bridge; the plan leaves a `tracing_subscriber::Layer` feeding exporters as an optional addition to `kardia-logger-console`."],[6935,"There is no in-place overwrite of a bound value. To change what consumers see, provide a new value (withdraw and re-provide, restart or update the provider), so that the change is observed."],[7884,"There is no loader-specific code: the loader imports plugins through the same internal ESM loader, so their modules are in the cache hmr inspects. hmr replaces plugins at the **registry** level, and loader entries keep their fibers."],[7391,"These packages are not described in the paper; they show how ordinary services are built on the core primitives."],[8392,"These pages describe Cordis, not kardia. Names such as `Context`, `Fiber`, `ctx.effect` and paths such as `cordis/packages/core/src/fiber.ts` refer to the TypeScript code. How kardia maps them to Rust is 